</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Duke University
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Surbhi Goel
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>University of Pennsylvania<span id="id2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">{yufa.zhou,yixiao.wang,anru.zhang}@duke.edu, surbhig@cis.upenn.edu</span>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Anru R. Zhang
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Duke University
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Time series forecasting (TSF) remains a challenging and largely unsolved problem in machine learning, despite significant recent efforts leveraging Large Language Models (LLMs), which predominantly rely on Transformer architectures.
Empirical evidence consistently shows that even powerful Transformers often fail to outperform much simpler models, e.g., linear models, on TSF tasks; however, a rigorous theoretical understanding of this phenomenon remains limited.
In this paper, we provide a theoretical analysis of Transformers’ limitations for TSF through the lens of In-Context Learning (ICL) theory.
Specifically, under AR(<math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>) data, we establish that: (1) Linear Self-Attention (LSA) models <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_italic">cannot</span> achieve lower expected MSE than classical linear models for in-context forecasting; (2) as the context length approaches to infinity, LSA asymptotically recovers the optimal linear predictor; and (3) under Chain-of-Thought (CoT) style inference, predictions collapse to the mean exponentially.
We empirically validate these findings through carefully designed experiments<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
          <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
          
          
          
          
          
          
          
        Code: <a href="https://github.com/MasterZhou1/ICL-Time-Series" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/MasterZhou1/ICL-Time-Series</a></span></span></span>.
Our theory not only sheds light on several previously underexplored phenomena but also offers practical insights for designing more effective forecasting architectures.
We hope our work encourages the broader research community to revisit the fundamental theoretical limitations of TSF and to critically evaluate the direct application of increasingly sophisticated architectures without deeper scrutiny.</p>
    
<blockquote id="abstract1.2" class="ltx_quote ltx_epigraph " style="width:345pt; margin-left:auto;;">
<div id="abstract1.2.1" class="ltx_block ltx_epigraph_text" style="text-align:right; ;">
<p id="abstract1.2.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="abstract1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">“The only thing we know about the future is that it will be different.”</span></p>
</div>
<div id="abstract1.2.2" class="ltx_block ltx_epigraph_source" style="border-top:solid 0pt; text-align:right; ;">
<p id="abstract1.2.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="abstract1.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:90%;">— Peter Drucker</span></p>
</div>
</blockquote>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Time series forecasting (TSF), a fundamental and longstanding challenge in machine learning, involves predicting future observations based on historical data <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>. TSF has broad applicability across diverse fields such as electronic health records, traffic analysis, energy consumption, and financial market predictions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib74" title="" class="ltx_ref">74</a>, <a href="#bib.bib75" title="" class="ltx_ref">75</a>]</cite>. In contrast, Transformers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib94" title="" class="ltx_ref">94</a>]</cite> have emerged as a cornerstone architecture in modern deep learning, achieving groundbreaking success across a wide array of sequence modeling tasks, including language modeling <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>, <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>, <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>, <a href="#bib.bib105" title="" class="ltx_ref">105</a>, <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>, computer vision <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib82" title="" class="ltx_ref">82</a>, <a href="#bib.bib72" title="" class="ltx_ref">72</a>]</cite>, visual-language modeling <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">61</a>, <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>]</cite>,
and video modeling <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Encouraged by their remarkable performance in language modeling, substantial efforts have been dedicated to adapting Transformers and Large Language Models (LLMs) to TSF <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib115" title="" class="ltx_ref">115</a>, <a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">68</a>, <a href="#bib.bib101" title="" class="ltx_ref">101</a>, <a href="#bib.bib111" title="" class="ltx_ref">111</a>, <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bib79" title="" class="ltx_ref">79</a>, <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>, <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite>. Nevertheless, empirical evidence consistently reveals that Transformer-based models frequently <span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">underperform</span> compared to simpler, linear forecasting methods, despite their quadratic time complexity and significantly larger parameter counts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib109" title="" class="ltx_ref">109</a>, <a href="#bib.bib91" title="" class="ltx_ref">91</a>, <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>, <a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>]</cite>. Such findings have prompted the development of lightweight linear models and frequency-domain approaches that typically outperform Transformers on long-horizon forecasting tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib103" title="" class="ltx_ref">103</a>, <a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">64</a>, <a href="#bib.bib104" title="" class="ltx_ref">104</a>, <a href="#bib.bib100" title="" class="ltx_ref">100</a>, <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>. However, a comprehensive theoretical understanding of why Transformers exhibit such limitations remains scarce.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Existing theoretical studies on Transformer for TSF mainly relied on Neural Tangent Kernel analyses or generic In-Context Learning (ICL) theory, yielding theoretical bounds often disconnected from practical relevance and failing to provide clear representational insights <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib85" title="" class="ltx_ref">85</a>, <a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">58</a>, <a href="#bib.bib97" title="" class="ltx_ref">97</a>]</cite>.
In sharp contrast, our work uniquely addresses the core representational limitations of Transformers through a rigorous theoretical examination grounded explicitly in classical Auto-Regressive (AR) models, fundamental frameworks dominating traditional TSF <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>.
By adopting Linear Self-Attention (LSA), a simplified yet powerful abstraction that eliminates the Softmax function for analytical tractability <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">73</a>, <a href="#bib.bib110" title="" class="ltx_ref">110</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib107" title="" class="ltx_ref">107</a>]</cite>, we uncover novel and essential constraints inherent in the attention mechanism itself.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Despite AR models’ inherent linearity rendering linear methods optimal, assessing the representational gap between optimal linear models and Transformers is a highly non-trivial endeavor. Under minimal assumptions—specifically, only assuming data adheres to a stable AR(<math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>) process—we establish substantial theoretical results that clearly delineate the representational boundaries of Transformers.
A related setting was studied in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">CLZZ [10]</a></cite>, which analyzes LSA on a one-dimensional linear dynamical system (a special case of AR(2)), while we generalize to AR(<math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>) under minimal stability assumptions, significantly increasing difficulty and scope.
Our findings indicate that even optimally parameterized LSA Transformers cannot outperform classical linear predictors in terms of expected MSE. With infinitely long historical context their predictions can theoretically converge to those of linear regression if training is sufficiently good, yet even this convergence arises not from any structural advantage of LSA but from the inherent stability of time series, which collapses their representational space to that of linear models on AR processes. In contrast, for any finite context length there exists a provable strictly positive gap, which diminishes at a rate no faster than <math id="S1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/n</annotation></semantics></math> as the context length grows.
We further analyze how predictions evolve and how errors accumulate under iterative Chain-of-Thought (CoT) inference.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Our theoretical analyses are complemented by empirical validations, providing practical insights into Transformer architectural design and clarifying their fundamental limitations in TSF contexts. Ultimately, our work calls for a reconsideration of the suitability and effectiveness of naively applying complex Transformer-based architectures to TSF. We advocate for deeper theoretical exploration to systematically unravel the foundational differences driving Transformers’ divergent performance across domains, bridging the gap between representational capability and practical efficacy.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Our primary contributions are summarized as follows:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We show that linear self-attention is essentially a restricted/compressed representation of linear regression, so it cannot outperform linear predictors (<a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Feature-Space View ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We establish a strictly positive performance gap between LSA and linear predictor, and proves that this gap vanishes at a rate no faster than <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/n</annotation></semantics></math> as context length increases (<a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 A Strict Finite-Sample Gap (Core Result) ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We characterize prediction behavior and error compounding rate in Chain-of-Thought inference, highlighting fundamental limitations of iterative Transformer predictions (<a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 Chain-of-Thought Rollout: Multi-Step Collapse ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We empirically corroborate our theoretical findings, offering insights into architectural implications and emphasizing the inherent representational limitations of Transformers for TSF (<a href="#S4" title="4 Numerical Verification ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>).</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<section id="S1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.1 </span>Related Work</h3>

<section id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Negative Results of Transformers on TSF.</h5>

<div id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Empirical studies consistently find Transformer- and LLM-based models struggle to surpass simpler linear baselines for time series forecasting. Language modeling components add minimal value <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib91" title="" class="ltx_ref">91</a>]</cite>, and linear variants (NLinear, DLinear) outperform Transformers on long-horizon benchmarks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib109" title="" class="ltx_ref">109</a>]</cite>. Analyses further question the utility of self-attention <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>]</cite>, show limited gains from scaling model size <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>]</cite>, and motivate lightweight methods such as FITS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib103" title="" class="ltx_ref">103</a>]</cite>, RLinear <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">64</a>]</cite>, OLinear <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib104" title="" class="ltx_ref">104</a>]</cite>, TimeMixer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib100" title="" class="ltx_ref">100</a>]</cite>, and CNN-based TSLANet <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>. Beyond forecasting, Transformers also struggle with zero-shot temporal reasoning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib77" title="" class="ltx_ref">77</a>]</cite> and anomaly detection tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib114" title="" class="ltx_ref">114</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Existing theoretical explanations remain limited.
Kernel-based analyses attribute Transformer failures to asymmetric feature learning within a Neural Tangent Kernel regime <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>]</cite>, but their synthetic assumptions and lack of universal lower bounds restrict their practical applicability <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>]</cite>. Similarly, recent In-Context Learning theory for linear dynamical systems provides a data-dependent lower bound largely reflecting intrinsic noise rather than representational shortcomings <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>.
In contrast, our work studies general AR(<math id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>) processes, employs distinct analytic techniques, and explicitly identifies representational constraints of Transformers relative to classical linear forecasters.
Due to the space constraint, we leave more related work to <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A More Related Work ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Preliminaries</h2>

<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Notations.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We write <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[n]:=\{1,2,\dots,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[n]:=\{1,2,\dots,n\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in\mdmathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in\mdmathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=(a_{1},\dots,a_{d})^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=(a_{1},\dots,a_{d})^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=[x_{1},\dots,x_{n}]\in\mdmathbb{R}^{m\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=[x_{1},\dots,x_{n}]\in\mdmathbb{R}^{m\times n}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}\in\mdmathbb{R}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}\in\mdmathbb{R}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math> is the <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th column; <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="X_{i,:}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>:</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{i,:}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="X_{:,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">:</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{:,j}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th row and <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>-th column; <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m11" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="X_{a:b,:}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>:</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{a:b,:}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m12" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="X_{:,c:d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">:</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{:,c:d}</annotation></semantics></math> denote row/column submatrices.
<math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{1}_{d},\mathbf{0}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{1}_{d},\mathbf{0}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{1}_{d\times d},\mathbf{0}_{d\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{1}_{d\times d},\mathbf{0}_{d\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> are all-ones/all-zeros vectors and matrices; <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> is the <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-identity.
<math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m17" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\|\cdot\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∥</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em">∥</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\cdot\|</annotation></semantics></math> denotes certain norm for a vector or matrix.
For symmetric <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A,B\in\mdmathbb{R}^{d\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A,B\in\mdmathbb{R}^{d\times d}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\succeq B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⪰</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\succeq B</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\succ B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>≻</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\succ B</annotation></semantics></math>) iff <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A-B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A-B</annotation></semantics></math> is positive semidefinite (definite).
For a sequence <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{a_{t}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{a_{t}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{t}\nearrow a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>↗</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{t}\nearrow a</annotation></semantics></math> means <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> increases monotonically to <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>.
For <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\in\mdmathbb{R}^{m\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\in\mdmathbb{R}^{m\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\in\mdmathbb{R}^{p\times q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\in\mdmathbb{R}^{p\times q}</annotation></semantics></math>, the Kronecker product <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\otimes B\in\mdmathbb{R}^{mp\times nq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\otimes B\in\mdmathbb{R}^{mp\times nq}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies
<math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(A\otimes B)_{(i-1)p+k,\,(j-1)q+\ell}:=A_{i,j}\,B_{k,\ell}\ (i\in[m],\,j\in[n],\,k\in[p],\,\ell\in[q])." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(A\otimes B)_{(i-1)p+k,\,(j-1)q+\ell}:=A_{i,j}\,B_{k,\ell}\ (i\in[m],\,j\in[n],\,k\in[p],\,\ell\in[q]).</annotation></semantics></math>
For <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\in\mdmathbb{R}^{p\times p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\in\mdmathbb{R}^{p\times p}</annotation></semantics></math> symmetric, <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{vech}(X)\in\mdmathbb{R}^{p(p+1)/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>vech</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{vech}(X)\in\mdmathbb{R}^{p(p+1)/2}</annotation></semantics></math> stacks the lower triangle (including diagonal); for <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\in\mdmathbb{R}^{m\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\in\mdmathbb{R}^{m\times n}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{vec}(X)\in\mdmathbb{R}^{mn}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>vec</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{vec}(X)\in\mdmathbb{R}^{mn}</annotation></semantics></math> stacks columns.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Time Series</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We begin by formally defining the notion of time series considered in this paper.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.1</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Time Series)<span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A <em id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">time series</em> is a finite sequence of random variables <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{x_{t}\}_{t=1}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{x_{t}\}_{t=1}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, indexed by discrete time <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in\{1,\dots,T\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in\{1,\dots,T\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We write <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="x_{1:T}:=(x_{1},\dots,x_{T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1:T}:=(x_{1},\dots,x_{T})</annotation></semantics></math> for the full sequence.
The process is called <em id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">multivariate</em> if each <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}\in\mdmathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}\in\mdmathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, and <em id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">univariate</em> if <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=1</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we primarily focus on <span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">univariate</span> Auto-Regressive processes, particularly the AR(<math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>) model, a cornerstone of classical time series analysis <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.2</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(AR(<math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>) Process <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>)<span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A real-valued stochastic process <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{x_{i}\}_{i=1}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{x_{i}\}_{i=1}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> follows an autoregressive model of order <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted AR(<math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>), if there exist coefficients <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{1},\dots,\rho_{p}\in\mdmathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{1},\dots,\rho_{p}\in\mdmathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> and white noise <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon_{i}\overset{\text{i.i.d.}}{\sim}\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma_{\varepsilon}^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">"</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo>∼</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic" mathsize="0.700em">i.i.d.</mtext></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">"</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon_{i}\overset{\text{i.i.d.}}{\sim}\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma_{\varepsilon}^{2})</annotation></semantics></math> such that for all <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S2.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle x_{i+1}=\sum_{j=1}^{p}\rho_{j}\,x_{i-j+1}+\varepsilon_{i+1}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>p</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">"</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle x_{i+1}=\sum_{j=1}^{p}\rho_{j}\,x_{i-j+1}+\varepsilon_{i+1},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">with fixed initial values <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{x_{-p+1},\dots,x_{0}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{x_{-p+1},\dots,x_{0}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Assuming the characteristic polynomial <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\rho_{1}z-\dots-\rho_{p}z^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\rho_{1}z-\dots-\rho_{p}z^{p}</annotation></semantics></math> has all roots outside the unit circle, i.e. <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|z|&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|z|&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, to ensure weak stationarity, the process satisfies:
(1) <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mdmathbb{E}[x_{i}]=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mdmathbb{E}[x_{i}]=0</annotation></semantics></math>,
(2) <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m11" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mdmathbb{E}[x_{i}^{2}]={}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mdmathbb{E}[x_{i}^{2}]={}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, and
(3) <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m12" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mdmathbb{E}[x_{i}x_{n+1}]={}_{n+1-i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mdmathbb{E}[x_{i}x_{n+1}]={}_{n+1-i}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m13" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{k}:=\mdmathbb{E}[x_{i}x_{i+k}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mmultiscripts><mo>:=</mo><mprescripts></mprescripts><mi>k</mi><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{k}:=\mdmathbb{E}[x_{i}x_{i+k}]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m14" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="r_{k}:={}_{k}/{}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mo rspace="0em">:=</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em">/</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{k}:={}_{k}/{}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Further classical results—including the ordinary least squares (OLS) solution for AR models, as well as the formulation and properties of linear predictors—are deferred to <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Time Series Fundamentals ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Transformer Architecture</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For theoretical tractability, we adopt the <span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Linear Self-Attention (LSA)</span>, which
omits Softmax and
has been widely used in prior theoretical works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib93" title="" class="ltx_ref">93</a>, <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib110" title="" class="ltx_ref">110</a>, <a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">73</a>, <a href="#bib.bib95" title="" class="ltx_ref">95</a>, <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>, <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>, <a href="#bib.bib88" title="" class="ltx_ref">88</a>, <a href="#bib.bib113" title="" class="ltx_ref">113</a>]</cite>, with growing empirical interest <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>, <a href="#bib.bib87" title="" class="ltx_ref">87</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib107" title="" class="ltx_ref">107</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.3</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Linear Self-Attention (LSA))<span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H\in\mdmathbb{R}^{(d+1)\times(m+1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H\in\mdmathbb{R}^{(d+1)\times(m+1)}</annotation></semantics></math> be the input matrix and define the causal mask
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M:=\begin{bmatrix}I_{m}&amp;0\\
0&amp;0\end{bmatrix}\in\mdmathbb{R}^{(m+1)\times(m+1)}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M:=\begin{bmatrix}I_{m}&amp;0\\
0&amp;0\end{bmatrix}\in\mdmathbb{R}^{(m+1)\times(m+1)}.</annotation></semantics></math>
We denote the attention weights <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P,Q\in\mdmathbb{R}^{(d+1)\times(d+1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P,Q\in\mdmathbb{R}^{(d+1)\times(d+1)}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then the linear self-attention output is defined as</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\sf LSA}(H):=H+\frac{1}{m}PHM(H^{\top}QH)\in\mdmathbb{R}^{(d+1)\times(m+1)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝖫𝖲𝖠</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>m</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sf LSA}(H):=H+\frac{1}{m}PHM(H^{\top}QH)\in\mdmathbb{R}^{(d+1)\times(m+1)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Throughout this paper, we focus on LSA-only Transformers.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.4</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<math id="S2.Thmtheorem4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-Layer LSA-Only Transformer)<span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\sf LSA}_{1},\dots,{\sf LSA}_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖫𝖲𝖠</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝖫𝖲𝖠</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sf LSA}_{1},\dots,{\sf LSA}_{L}</annotation></semantics></math> be a sequence of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> linear self-attention layers as defined in <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem3" title="Definition 2.3 (Linear Self-Attention (LSA)). ‣ 2.2 Transformer Architecture ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Definition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a>. The <math id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-layer Transformer is defined recursively via function composition:</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\sf TF}(H):={\sf LSA}_{L}\circ{\sf LSA}_{L-1}\circ\dots\circ{\sf LSA}_{1}(H)\in\mdmathbb{R}^{(d+1)\times(m+1)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝖳𝖥</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝖫𝖲𝖠</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>𝖫𝖲𝖠</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝖫𝖲𝖠</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sf TF}(H):={\sf LSA}_{L}\circ{\sf LSA}_{L-1}\circ\dots\circ{\sf LSA}_{1}(H)\in\mdmathbb{R}^{(d+1)\times(m+1)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3 </span>In-Context Time Series Forecasting</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given a univariate sequence <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="x_{1:n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1:n}</annotation></semantics></math> of AR order <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> (<a href="#S2.Thmtheorem2" title="Definition 2.2 (AR(𝑝) Process []). ‣ 2.1 Time Series ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Definition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>), we build a Hankel matrix <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{n}\in\mdmathbb{R}^{(p+1)\times(n-p+1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{n}\in\mdmathbb{R}^{(p+1)\times(n-p+1)}</annotation></semantics></math> (<a href="#S2.Thmtheorem5" title="Definition 2.5 (Hankel Matrix). ‣ 2.3 In-Context Time Series Forecasting ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Definition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.5</span></a>) whose final column is zero-padded in its last entry as a <em id="S2.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">label slot</em> for <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{n+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{n+1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Setting <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=p</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=n-p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=n-p</annotation></semantics></math>, we feed <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> into the <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-layer LSA-only Transformer <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\sf TF}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖳𝖥</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sf TF}</annotation></semantics></math> (<a href="#S2.Thmtheorem4" title="Definition 2.4 (𝐿-Layer LSA-Only Transformer). ‣ 2.2 Transformer Architecture ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Definition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a>) and read the forecast directly from the label slot:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{x}_{n+1}:=[{\sf TF}(H_{n})]_{(p+1,\;n-p+1)}\in\mdmathbb{R}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>:=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>𝖳𝖥</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{x}_{n+1}:=[{\sf TF}(H_{n})]_{(p+1,\;n-p+1)}\in\mdmathbb{R}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The Hankel construction (<a href="#S2.Thmtheorem5" title="Definition 2.5 (Hankel Matrix). ‣ 2.3 In-Context Time Series Forecasting ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Definition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.5</span></a>) encodes the autoregressive structure in context: identifying an AR(<math id="S2.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>) process requires at least <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> lags, and each column of the Hankel matrix provides exactly this information.
Unlike raw token sequences, the Hankel layout already fixes the relative order of observations, so it implicitly carries position encoding. This both respects the time-series autoregressive dependencies and avoids the need for additional positional embeddings that can make Transformers harder to train.
The formal justification is given in <a href="#A5.SS3" title="E.3 Nested Transformer Feature Spaces and Risk Monotonicity in Time Series Prediction ‣ Appendix E Transformers Expressivity for TSF ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.5</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Hankel Matrix)<span id="S2.Thmtheorem5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{1},\dots,x_{n})\in\mdmathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{1},\dots,x_{n})\in\mdmathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\leq n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\leq n</annotation></semantics></math>, define</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{n}:=\begin{bmatrix}x_{1}&amp;x_{2}&amp;\cdots&amp;x_{n-p}&amp;x_{n-p+1}\\
x_{2}&amp;x_{3}&amp;\cdots&amp;x_{n-p+1}&amp;x_{n-p+2}\\
\vdots&amp;\vdots&amp;&amp;\vdots&amp;\vdots\\
x_{p}&amp;x_{p+1}&amp;\cdots&amp;x_{n-1}&amp;x_{n}\\
x_{p+1}&amp;x_{p+2}&amp;\cdots&amp;x_{n}&amp;0\end{bmatrix}\in\mdmathbb{R}^{(p+1)\times(n-p+1)}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mtd><mtd><mo>⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mtd><mtd><mo>⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><mo>⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><mo>⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{n}:=\begin{bmatrix}x_{1}&amp;x_{2}&amp;\cdots&amp;x_{n-p}&amp;x_{n-p+1}\\
x_{2}&amp;x_{3}&amp;\cdots&amp;x_{n-p+1}&amp;x_{n-p+2}\\
\vdots&amp;\vdots&amp;&amp;\vdots&amp;\vdots\\
x_{p}&amp;x_{p+1}&amp;\cdots&amp;x_{n-1}&amp;x_{n}\\
x_{p+1}&amp;x_{p+2}&amp;\cdots&amp;x_{n}&amp;0\end{bmatrix}\in\mdmathbb{R}^{(p+1)\times(n-p+1)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where each column is a sliding window of length <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p{+}1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p{+}1</annotation></semantics></math>, with the last zero marking the prediction.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Main Results</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We organize our theoretical contributions into three parts. First, in <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Feature-Space View ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> we provide a high-level feature-space perspective: By Hankelizing the input and analyzing the induced <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>-algebra, we show that one-layer linear self-attention (LSA) effectively compresses history into a restricted cubic feature class which asymptotically collapses to the last <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> lags, thereby anticipating a structural disadvantage relative to linear regression (LR). Building on this intuition, <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 A Strict Finite-Sample Gap (Core Result) ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> establishes our core result: for autoregressive (AR) and more generally linear stationary processes, the optimal one-layer LSA predictor suffers a <em id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">strict finite-sample excess risk</em> over LR, quantified by a positive Schur–complement gap that vanishes only asymptotically at an explicit <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/n</annotation></semantics></math> rate. While stacking additional LSA layers yields monotone improvements, LR remains the fundamental benchmark that cannot be surpassed. Finally, <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 Chain-of-Thought Rollout: Multi-Step Collapse ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> turns to multistep forecasting: we prove that chain-of-thought (CoT) rollout, in stark contrast to its benefits in language tasks, compounds errors exponentially and collapses forecasts to the mean, with LSA uniformly dominated by LR at every horizon.
All formal proofs are deferred to <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Transformers Expressivity for TSF ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendices</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a>, <a href="#A6" title="Appendix F Closed-Form Gap Characterization between LSA and Linear Models ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F</span></a>, <a href="#A7" title="Appendix G Chain-of-Thought (CoT) Rollout in TSF: Collapse-to-Mean and Error Compounding ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">G</span></a> and <a href="#A8" title="Appendix H De–Gaussifying the Gap: Linear Stationary Processes ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">H</span></a>. In contrast to prior work that mainly studies LR in ICL settings <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib110" title="" class="ltx_ref">110</a>]</cite>, time series settings introduce intrinsic temporal dependencies among input variables, making the analysis substantially more complex and non-trivial.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Feature-Space View</h3>

<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Restricted feature class.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We first reparameterize <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P,Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P,Q</annotation></semantics></math> in <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem3" title="Definition 2.3 (Linear Self-Attention (LSA)). ‣ 2.2 Transformer Architecture ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Definition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a> to <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A,b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A,b</annotation></semantics></math> to obtain the simplified form of the LSA prediction (<a href="#A5.Thmtheorem3" title="Lemma E.3 (Closed-form readout of one-layer LSA on Hankel input []). ‣ E.1 Fundamentals of Linear Self-Attention ‣ Appendix E Transformers Expressivity for TSF ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.3</span></a>).
Let <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi=\Phi(H_{n};A,b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi=\Phi(H_{n};A,b)</annotation></semantics></math> denote the one-layer LSA features induced by query–key weighting and value aggregation. The predictor admits a cubic lifting:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Cubic lifting for one-layer LSA)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There exist coefficients <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\beta_{j,r,k}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\beta_{j,r,k}\}</annotation></semantics></math> such that</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\widehat{x}^{\mathrm{LSA}}_{n+1}(A,b)\;=\;\sum_{j,r,k}\beta_{j,r,k}\,\varphi^{(p)}_{j,r,k}(x_{1:n}),\qquad\varphi^{(p)}_{j,r,k}\ \text{are degree-3 monomials in }\{x_{t}\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>LSA</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.391em">=</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">'</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">'</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">are degree-3 monomials in </mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{x}^{\mathrm{LSA}}_{n+1}(A,b)\;=\;\sum_{j,r,k}\beta_{j,r,k}\,\varphi^{(p)}_{j,r,k}(x_{1:n}),\qquad\varphi^{(p)}_{j,r,k}\ \text{are degree-3 monomials in }\{x_{t}\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Hence one-layer LSA is a linear functional over a cubic feature space <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{(p)}_{\mathrm{LSA}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>LSA</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{(p)}_{\mathrm{LSA}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Proof deferred to <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Transformers Expressivity for TSF ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a>.</em>
<a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma 3.1 (Cubic lifting for one-layer LSA). ‣ Restricted feature class. ‣ 3.1 Feature-Space View ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> shows that the LSA readout lives in a <em id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">cubic</em> feature space of the raw inputs.
By the <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-projection property of conditional expectation (orthogonality onto sub-<math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>-algebras), we obtain <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem2" title="Proposition 3.2 (Information monotonicity). ‣ Restricted feature class. ‣ 3.1 Feature-Space View ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>: any predictor operating on <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> cannot achieve lower MSE than the optimal predictor operating on the full context <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="x_{1:n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1:n}</annotation></semantics></math>. In short, the attention-derived representation is <em id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">informationally coarser</em> than the raw context; adding architectural complexity does not reveal additional predictive signal beyond the last <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> lags, but only reweights existing information.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.2</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Information monotonicity)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\sigma(\Phi)\subseteq\sigma(x_{1:n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(\Phi)\subseteq\sigma(x_{1:n})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">. Hence, by conditional orthogonality/Jensen,</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\inf_{f}\ \mdmathbb E\left[(x_{n+1}-f(x_{1:n}))^{2}\right]\ \leq\ \inf_{g}\ \mdmathbb E\left[(x_{n+1}-g(\Phi))^{2}\right]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">inf</mo><mi>f</mi></munder><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo rspace="0.500em">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.500em">≤</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">inf</mo><mi>g</mi></munder><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\inf_{f}\ \mdmathbb E\left[(x_{n+1}-f(x_{1:n}))^{2}\right]\ \leq\ \inf_{g}\ \mdmathbb E\left[(x_{n+1}-g(\Phi))^{2}\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Proof deferred to <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Transformers Expressivity for TSF ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a>.</em>
Furthermore, <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="Proposition 3.3 (Asymptotic collapse of LSA features). ‣ Restricted feature class. ‣ 3.1 Feature-Space View ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> formalizes the asymptotic picture: as the number of observed contexts <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, the <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p{+}1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p{+}1)</annotation></semantics></math>-row Hankel design ensures access to exactly the <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> relevant lags; by ergodicity, empirical Hankel Gram blocks converge to their Toeplitz limits, cross-row correlations stabilize, and the cubic coordinates concentrate onto the last-<math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>-lag subspace.
Consequently, one-layer LSA <em id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">cannot outperform</em> LR and can at best <em id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">match it asymptotically</em> (<a href="#A5.Thmtheorem14" title="Proposition E.14 (Asymptotic exact recovery: a constructive parameter choice). ‣ Setup. ‣ E.3 Nested Transformer Feature Spaces and Risk Monotonicity in Time Series Prediction ‣ Appendix E Transformers Expressivity for TSF ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.14</span></a>).
See <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Transformers Expressivity for TSF ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a> for an optimal LSA parameter choice achieving this limit.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.3</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Asymptotic collapse of LSA features)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">As <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math> for a stable AR(<math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>), the coordinates <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\varphi^{(p)}_{j,r,k}(x_{1:n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">'</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi^{(p)}_{j,r,k}(x_{1:n})</annotation></semantics></math> converge in <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> to scaled copies of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{x_{n-p+1},\dots,x_{n}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{x_{n-p+1},\dots,x_{n}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus the optimal one-layer LSA readout asymptotically reduces to a linear function of the last <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> lags.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="136.05" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 650 136.05" width="650"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,136.05) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#CCCCFF;" fill="#CCCCFF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 9.26 L 0 126.79 C 0 131.9 4.14 136.05 9.26 136.05 L 640.74 136.05 C 645.86 136.05 650 131.9 650 126.79 L 650 9.26 C 650 4.14 645.86 0 640.74 0 L 9.26 0 C 4.14 0 0 4.14 0 9.26 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F5F5F5;" fill="#F5F5F5" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.38 9.26 L 1.38 113.11 L 648.62 113.11 L 648.62 9.26 C 648.62 4.91 645.09 1.38 640.74 1.38 L 9.26 1.38 C 4.91 1.38 1.38 4.91 1.38 9.26 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 9.26 121.12)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:39.69em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="12.3" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="549.2"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:39.69em;">
<span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Takeaway 1: Attention is Not All You Need for TSF</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 9.26 12.72)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:45.64em;--ltx-fo-height:6.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="95.98" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 92.52)" width="631.53"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.pic1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:45.64em;">
<span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.pic1.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.pic1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Feature-Space.<span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.pic1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> LSA operates on a strictly coarser <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">�</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>-algebra than the raw context; it can at best reweight the last-<math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.pic1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> lags and cannot unlock signal beyond them. For time series with pronounced linear structure (e.g., AR/ARMA), this is a <em id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.pic1.2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">fundamental representational limitation</em>. This aligns with empirical findings that simple linear models often outperform Transformers in TSF <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib109" title="" class="ltx_ref">109</a>, <a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>, <a href="#bib.bib91" title="" class="ltx_ref">91</a>, <a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">70</a>]</cite>.</span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>A Strict Finite-Sample Gap (Core Result)</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section we rigorously establish and quantitatively characterize the <em id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">fundamental representational limitation</em> of one-layer LSA.
Our main technical device is a <em id="S3.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Kronecker-product lifting</em> of the Hankel-derived features: the one-layer LSA readout can be written as</p>
<table id="S3.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\widehat{x}^{\mathrm{LSA}}_{n+1}=\widetilde{\eta}^{\top}Z,\qquad Z:=\big(\vech G\big)\otimes x,\ \ x:=x_{n-p+1:n}\in\mdmathbb R^{p}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>LSA</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mi>Z</mi><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>vech</mi><mi>G</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{x}^{\mathrm{LSA}}_{n+1}=\widetilde{\eta}^{\top}Z,\qquad Z:=\big(\vech G\big)\otimes x,\ \ x:=x_{n-p+1:n}\in\mdmathbb R^{p},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="G=G(x_{1:n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=G(x_{1:n})</annotation></semantics></math> is the Hankel Gram matrix, <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vech(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>vech</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vech(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes half-vectorization, and <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\eta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\eta}</annotation></semantics></math> is an affine reparameterization of <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(A,b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(A,b)</annotation></semantics></math> (see <a href="#A6.SS2" title="F.2 A finite-sample optimality gap for one-layer LSA ‣ Appendix F Closed-Form Gap Characterization between LSA and Linear Models ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F.2</span></a>).
This lifts the <span id="S3.SS2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">cubic</span> dependence of LSA into an ordinary <span id="S3.SS2.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">linear regression</span> in the lifted space.
Let <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\in\mdmathbb R^{qp}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\in\mdmathbb R^{qp}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q:=\dim(\vech G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo rspace="0.1389em">:=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>vech</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q:=\dim(\vech G)</annotation></semantics></math>.
We further define <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{S}:=\mdmathbb E[ZZ^{\top}],\ \widetilde{r}:=\mdmathbb E[Zx^{\top}],\ \Gamma_{p}:=\mdmathbb E[xx^{\top}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>Z</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{S}:=\mdmathbb E[ZZ^{\top}],\ \widetilde{r}:=\mdmathbb E[Zx^{\top}],\ \Gamma_{p}:=\mdmathbb E[xx^{\top}]</annotation></semantics></math>, and the induced Schur complement is <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{n}:=\Gamma_{p}-\widetilde{r}^{\top}\widetilde{S}^{-1}\widetilde{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{n}:=\Gamma_{p}-\widetilde{r}^{\top}\widetilde{S}^{-1}\widetilde{r}</annotation></semantics></math>. Intuitively, <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> captures the component of the linear signal in the last <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> lags that remains <em id="S3.SS2.p1.2.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">orthogonal</em> to the span of lifted LSA features—namely, the exact <em id="S3.SS2.p1.2.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">representation gap</em> characterized in <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 3.4 (Strict finite-sample gap: AR(𝑝)). ‣ 3.2 A Strict Finite-Sample Gap (Core Result) ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>. Moreover, we prove in <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 3.4 (Strict finite-sample gap: AR(𝑝)). ‣ 3.2 A Strict Finite-Sample Gap (Core Result) ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a> that <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{n}\succ 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>≻</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{n}\succ 0</annotation></semantics></math> for any finite <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, establishing a strict finite-sample gap. We then derive an explicit first-order expansion <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{n}=\frac{1}{n}B_{p}+o(1/n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{n}=\frac{1}{n}B_{p}+o(1/n)</annotation></semantics></math> under Gaussianity in <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 3.5 (Explicit 1/𝑛 rate: Gaussian). ‣ First-order gap rate under Gaussianity. ‣ 3.2 A Strict Finite-Sample Gap (Core Result) ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a>, and show in <a href="#A8.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem H.3 (Strict finite–sample gap under linear stationarity). ‣ Linear regression predictor. ‣ Appendix H De–Gaussifying the Gap: Linear Stationary Processes ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorems</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">H.3</span></a>, <a href="#A8.Thmtheorem4" title="Lemma H.4 (Moment expansions via cumulants). ‣ Cumulant identities for linear processes. ‣ Appendix H De–Gaussifying the Gap: Linear Stationary Processes ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">H.4</span></a> and <a href="#A8.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem H.5 (Order of the non–Gaussian gap). ‣ (d) Collecting the pieces. ‣ Appendix H De–Gaussifying the Gap: Linear Stationary Processes ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">H.5</span></a> that both the strictness and the <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/n</annotation></semantics></math> rate persist for general linear stationary processes, with the leading constant adjusted by cumulant spectra (<a href="#A8" title="Appendix H De–Gaussifying the Gap: Linear Stationary Processes ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">H</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.4</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Strict finite-sample gap: AR(<math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>))<span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq p</annotation></semantics></math> and stable AR(<math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>),</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\min_{A,b}\ \mdmathbb E\left[(\widehat{x}^{\mathrm{LSA}}_{n+1}-x_{n+1})^{2}\right]\ \geq\ \min_{w}\ \mdmathbb E\left[(w^{\top}x_{n-p+1:n}-x_{n+1})^{2}\right]\;+\;\rho^{\top}\Delta_{n}\rho,\qquad\Delta_{n}\succ 0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></munder><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>LSA</mi></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo rspace="0.500em">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.778em">≥</mo><munder><mi>min</mi><mi>w</mi></munder><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo rspace="0.280em">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.502em">+</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>≻</mo><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min_{A,b}\ \mdmathbb E\left[(\widehat{x}^{\mathrm{LSA}}_{n+1}-x_{n+1})^{2}\right]\ \geq\ \min_{w}\ \mdmathbb E\left[(w^{\top}x_{n-p+1:n}-x_{n+1})^{2}\right]\;+\;\rho^{\top}\Delta_{n}\rho,\qquad\Delta_{n}\succ 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">What this says.</span>
Even after optimizing over all one-layer LSA parameters, the <em id="S3.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">best-in-class</em> LSA risk is strictly larger than the <em id="S3.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">best-in-class</em> linear risk by the explicit quadratic form <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho^{\top}\Delta_{n}\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho^{\top}\Delta_{n}\rho</annotation></semantics></math>; the gap is <em id="S3.SS2.p2.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">structural</em> (positive definite), not an estimation or optimization artifact.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof Sketch.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">(i) Since the lifted loss is a strictly convex quadratic in <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\eta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\eta}</annotation></semantics></math>, it has the unique minimizer <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\eta}^{*}=\widetilde{S}^{-1}\widetilde{r},\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\eta}^{*}=\widetilde{S}^{-1}\widetilde{r},\rho</annotation></semantics></math>, yielding the class optimum <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min_{\widetilde{\eta}}\mathcal{L}(\widetilde{\eta})=\sigma_{\varepsilon}^{2}+\rho^{\top}\Delta_{n}\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>min</mi><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">"</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min_{\widetilde{\eta}}\mathcal{L}(\widetilde{\eta})=\sigma_{\varepsilon}^{2}+\rho^{\top}\Delta_{n}\rho</annotation></semantics></math>.
(ii) Under AR(<math id="S3.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>), the optimal linear predictor attains the Bayes one-step risk <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\varepsilon}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">"</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\varepsilon}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, so the excess risk equals <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho^{\top}\Delta_{n}\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho^{\top}\Delta_{n}\rho</annotation></semantics></math>.
(iii) Strictness follows from block positive definiteness of the joint covariance</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mdmathbb E\left[\begin{pmatrix}Z\\
x\end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix}Z\\
x\end{pmatrix}^{\top}\right]=\begin{pmatrix}\widetilde{S}&amp;\widetilde{r}\\[2.0pt]
\widetilde{r}^{\top}&amp;\Gamma_{p}\end{pmatrix}\succ 0." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mi>Z</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>x</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mi>Z</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>x</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mtd><mtd><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mtd><mtd><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>≻</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mdmathbb E\left[\begin{pmatrix}Z\\
x\end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix}Z\\
x\end{pmatrix}^{\top}\right]=\begin{pmatrix}\widetilde{S}&amp;\widetilde{r}\\[2.0pt]
\widetilde{r}^{\top}&amp;\Gamma_{p}\end{pmatrix}\succ 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Specifically, we reduce the claim to showing that the (non-lifted) joint covariance of <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\vech G,x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>vech</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\vech G,x)</annotation></semantics></math> is positive definite. We prove this via an innovation-based elimination from the newest to older indices, establishing that no nontrivial linear combination can have zero variance. Using the Schur-complement property then yields <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{n}\succ 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>≻</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{n}\succ 0</annotation></semantics></math>.
All blocks admit closed forms as Hankel–Toeplitz moments up to orders 4 and 6 (from earlier definitions of <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{S},\widetilde{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{S},\widetilde{r}</annotation></semantics></math> in this section).
Hence, <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="S3.SS2.p4.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">computable</em> from process moments; in the Gaussian case these moments follow from Isserlis’ theorem. A warm-up AR(1) calculation is given in <a href="#A6.SS1" title="F.1 Warm Up via AR(1) ‣ Appendix F Closed-Form Gap Characterization between LSA and Linear Models ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F.1</span></a>.
See Appendix <a href="#A6.SS2" title="F.2 A finite-sample optimality gap for one-layer LSA ‣ Appendix F Closed-Form Gap Characterization between LSA and Linear Models ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F.2</span></a> for the complete proof.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">First-order gap rate under Gaussianity.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now quantify the finite-<math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> excess risk in <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 3.4 (Strict finite-sample gap: AR(𝑝)). ‣ 3.2 A Strict Finite-Sample Gap (Core Result) ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>. For zero-mean stationary Gaussian AR(<math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>), we expand the lifted moments around their population (rank-one) limit and evaluate the Schur complement via a singular block inverse.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.5</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Explicit <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/n</annotation></semantics></math> rate: Gaussian)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For Gaussian AR(<math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>),</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{n}=\Gamma_{p}-\widetilde{r}_{n}^{\top}\widetilde{S}_{n}^{-1}\widetilde{r}_{n}=\frac{1}{n}B_{p}+o(1/n),\qquad B_{p}\succeq 0\ \text{(generically }B_{p}\succ 0\text{)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>n</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>⪰</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">(generically </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≻</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">)</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{n}=\Gamma_{p}-\widetilde{r}_{n}^{\top}\widetilde{S}_{n}^{-1}\widetilde{r}_{n}=\frac{1}{n}B_{p}+o(1/n),\qquad B_{p}\succeq 0\ \text{(generically }B_{p}\succ 0\text{)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Consequently, for any fixed <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\rho\|\geq r&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\rho\|\geq r&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> there exists <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{r}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{r}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> such that</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\min_{A,b}\mdmathbb E[(\widehat{x}^{\mathrm{LSA}}_{n+1}-x_{n+1})^{2}]\ \geq\ \min_{w}\mdmathbb E[(w^{\top}x_{n-p+1:n}-x_{n+1})^{2}]\;+\;\frac{c_{r}}{n}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></munder><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>LSA</mi></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.778em">≥</mo><munder><mi>min</mi><mi>w</mi></munder><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.502em">+</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min_{A,b}\mdmathbb E[(\widehat{x}^{\mathrm{LSA}}_{n+1}-x_{n+1})^{2}]\ \geq\ \min_{w}\mdmathbb E[(w^{\top}x_{n-p+1:n}-x_{n+1})^{2}]\;+\;\frac{c_{r}}{n}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof Sketch.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">The argument proceeds in two steps:
<em id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">(1) Moment expansions.</em> We first expand the lifted covariance quantities using standard tools
(Isserlis’ theorem for Gaussian moments and Toeplitz summation for time averages).
This shows that the main term has a simple block-diagonal structure determined entirely by the process autocovariances, while the finite-sample corrections appear at order <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/n</annotation></semantics></math>.
<em id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">(2) Singular block inversion.</em> Because part of the leading block vanishes in the limit,
the inverse matrix develops a component that scales linearly with <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>.
Applying a first-order block-inverse expansion reveals that the excess-risk matrix <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> itself scales like <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/n</annotation></semantics></math>,
with a computable correction term depending on the second-order moment structure of the process.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">In short, the proof shows that after separating the dominant block structure and carefully controlling the inverse,
the residual term <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> has an explicit <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/n</annotation></semantics></math>-expansion governed entirely by process moments.
A complete proof is provided in Appendix <a href="#A6.SS3" title="F.3 Order of the finite-sample gap ‣ Appendix F Closed-Form Gap Characterization between LSA and Linear Models ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F.3</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3.6</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Why the rate is <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/n</annotation></semantics></math>)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem6.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u=\operatorname{vech}(\Gamma_{p+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>vech</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u=\operatorname{vech}(\Gamma_{p+1})</annotation></semantics></math>. At the population limit <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{S}_{\infty}=(uu^{\top})\otimes\Gamma_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{S}_{\infty}=(uu^{\top})\otimes\Gamma_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> is rank-one along <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math>. Finite <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> introduces <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta(1/n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta(1/n)</annotation></semantics></math> perturbations that regularize the orthogonal directions, so the Schur complement <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{p}-\widetilde{r}_{n}^{\top}\widetilde{S}_{n}^{-1}\widetilde{r}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>n</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{p}-\widetilde{r}_{n}^{\top}\widetilde{S}_{n}^{-1}\widetilde{r}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta(1/n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta(1/n)</annotation></semantics></math>. The overlap of Hankel windows is the source of these first–order terms.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Beyond Gaussianity: strict gap and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/n</annotation></semantics></math> rate.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We work under <em id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">linear stationarity</em> with Wold representation <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}=\sum_{k\geq 0}{}_{k}\varepsilon_{t-k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mmultiscripts><mi mathvariant="normal">"</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}=\sum_{k\geq 0}{}_{k}\varepsilon_{t-k}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\sum_{k}|{}_{k}|&lt;\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em">&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{k}|{}_{k}|&lt;\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, and i.i.d. <em id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">symmetric</em> innovations <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\varepsilon_{t}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">"</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\varepsilon_{t}\}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mdmathbb E[\varepsilon_{t}]=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">"</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mdmathbb E[\varepsilon_{t}]=0</annotation></semantics></math>, possessing finite fourth and sixth moments. Replacing Isserlis’ theorem by the moment–cumulant formula preserves positive definiteness of the joint covariance, so the <span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">strictly positive</span> gap still holds (<a href="#A8.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem H.3 (Strict finite–sample gap under linear stationarity). ‣ Linear regression predictor. ‣ Appendix H De–Gaussifying the Gap: Linear Stationary Processes ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">H.3</span></a>); furthermore, the convergence rate remains <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/n</annotation></semantics></math> via the non-Gaussian expansions (<a href="#A8.Thmtheorem4" title="Lemma H.4 (Moment expansions via cumulants). ‣ Cumulant identities for linear processes. ‣ Appendix H De–Gaussifying the Gap: Linear Stationary Processes ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">H.4</span></a>) culminating in the rate result (<a href="#A8.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem H.5 (Order of the non–Gaussian gap). ‣ (d) Collecting the pieces. ‣ Appendix H De–Gaussifying the Gap: Linear Stationary Processes ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">H.5</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Multi-layer LSA yields monotone improvement.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Because the stacked Kronecker feature set enlarges with depth, the optimal LSA risk is monotone nonincreasing in <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> by projection isotonicity (<a href="#A6.Thmtheorem18" title="Proposition F.18 (Monotone improvement with depth). ‣ Depth helps: a simple embedding argument. ‣ F.4 A finite–sample gap for 𝐿 stacked LSA layers (with monotone improvement) ‣ Appendix F Closed-Form Gap Characterization between LSA and Linear Models ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F.18</span></a>, using the Moore–Penrose formulation <a href="#A6.E12" title="In A one–shot convex relaxation for depth 𝐿. ‣ F.4 A finite–sample gap for 𝐿 stacked LSA layers (with monotone improvement) ‣ Appendix F Closed-Form Gap Characterization between LSA and Linear Models ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equations</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a> and <a href="#A6.E13" title="Equation 13 ‣ A one–shot convex relaxation for depth 𝐿. ‣ F.4 A finite–sample gap for 𝐿 stacked LSA layers (with monotone improvement) ‣ Appendix F Closed-Form Gap Characterization between LSA and Linear Models ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a> and the residual-covariance view <a href="#A6.Thmtheorem17" title="Lemma F.17 (Why �_{𝑛,𝐿}⪰0 even if 𝑆̃_𝐿 is singular). ‣ A one–shot convex relaxation for depth 𝐿. ‣ F.4 A finite–sample gap for 𝐿 stacked LSA layers (with monotone improvement) ‣ Appendix F Closed-Form Gap Characterization between LSA and Linear Models ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F.17</span></a>). Moreover, if the <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(L{+}1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(L{+}1)</annotation></semantics></math>-st layer contributes any <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-nonredundant direction—equivalently <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Var}\big(P_{\mathcal{H}_{L}^{\perp}}(g^{(L)}\otimes x)\big)&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Var</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>L</mi><mo>⟂</mo></msubsup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Var}\big(P_{\mathcal{H}_{L}^{\perp}}(g^{(L)}\otimes x)\big)&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{L}=\mathrm{span}\{g^{(0)}\otimes x,\dots,g^{(L-1)}\otimes x\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>span</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{L}=\mathrm{span}\{g^{(0)}\otimes x,\dots,g^{(L-1)}\otimes x\}</annotation></semantics></math>—then the Moore–Penrose Schur complement strictly decreases (<a href="#A6.Thmtheorem18" title="Proposition F.18 (Monotone improvement with depth). ‣ Depth helps: a simple embedding argument. ‣ F.4 A finite–sample gap for 𝐿 stacked LSA layers (with monotone improvement) ‣ Appendix F Closed-Form Gap Characterization between LSA and Linear Models ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F.18</span></a>), yielding</p>
<table id="S3.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min_{\{b^{(\ell)},A^{(\ell)}\}_{\ell=0}^{L}}\mdmathbb E\big[(\widehat{x}_{n+1}^{(L+1)}-x_{n+1})^{2}\big]\;&lt;\;\min_{\{b^{(\ell)},A^{(\ell)}\}_{\ell=0}^{L-1}}\mdmathbb E\big[(\widehat{x}_{n+1}^{(L)}-x_{n+1})^{2}\big]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></msubsup></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.280em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">&lt;</mo><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min_{\{b^{(\ell)},A^{(\ell)}\}_{\ell=0}^{L}}\mdmathbb E\big[(\widehat{x}_{n+1}^{(L+1)}-x_{n+1})^{2}\big]\;&lt;\;\min_{\{b^{(\ell)},A^{(\ell)}\}_{\ell=0}^{L-1}}\mdmathbb E\big[(\widehat{x}_{n+1}^{(L)}-x_{n+1})^{2}\big].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3.7</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A potential misinterpretation is that we claim Transformers cannot solve or fit time series at all. This is NOT the case. Rather, our result shows that Transformers cannot outperform simple linear models beyond a certain extent. While they may be capable of solving time series tasks to some degree, their performance does not substantially exceed that of linear models.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="285.6" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 650 285.6" width="650"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,285.6) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#CCCCFF;" fill="#CCCCFF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 9.26 L 0 276.34 C 0 281.46 4.14 285.6 9.26 285.6 L 640.74 285.6 C 645.86 285.6 650 281.46 650 276.34 L 650 9.26 C 650 4.14 645.86 0 640.74 0 L 9.26 0 C 4.14 0 0 4.14 0 9.26 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F5F5F5;" fill="#F5F5F5" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.38 9.26 L 1.38 262.66 L 648.62 262.66 L 648.62 9.26 C 648.62 4.91 645.09 1.38 640.74 1.38 L 9.26 1.38 C 4.91 1.38 1.38 4.91 1.38 9.26 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 9.26 270.67)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:39.69em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="12.3" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="549.2"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:39.69em;">
<span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Takeaway 2: Strictly Positive Gap between LSA and Linear Predictor</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 9.26 12.72)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:45.64em;--ltx-fo-height:17.49em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="245.53" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 242.07)" width="631.53"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.pic1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:45.64em;">
<span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.pic1.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.pic1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Strictness.<span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.pic1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> For any finite <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, one-layer LSA has a <em id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.pic1.2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">strict</em> excess risk over <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.pic1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>-lag LR equal to <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.pic1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho^{\top}\Delta_{n}\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">�</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho^{\top}\Delta_{n}\rho</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.pic1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{n}\succ 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">≻</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{n}\succ 0</annotation></semantics></math> (<a href="#S3.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 3.4 (Strict finite-sample gap: AR(𝑝)). ‣ 3.2 A Strict Finite-Sample Gap (Core Result) ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>).</span></span></span>
<span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.pic1.2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.pic1.2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Rate.<span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.pic1.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> The gap admits an explicit <em id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.pic1.2.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.pic1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">/</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/n</annotation></semantics></math></em> expansion with PSD leading constant <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.pic1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">B</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> (generically PD) (<a href="#S3.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 3.5 (Explicit 1/𝑛 rate: Gaussian). ‣ First-order gap rate under Gaussianity. ‣ 3.2 A Strict Finite-Sample Gap (Core Result) ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a>).</span></span></span>
<span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.pic1.2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.pic1.2.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Robustness.<span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.pic1.2.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Under linear stationarity with finite moments, strictness and the <em id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.pic1.2.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.pic1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">/</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/n</annotation></semantics></math></em> rate persist; non-Gaussianity only alters the constant via cumulants (<a href="#A8.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem H.3 (Strict finite–sample gap under linear stationarity). ‣ Linear regression predictor. ‣ Appendix H De–Gaussifying the Gap: Linear Stationary Processes ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorems</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">H.3</span></a>, <a href="#A8.Thmtheorem6" title="Lemma H.6 (Moment–cumulant formula). ‣ Auxiliary lemmas used in ‣ Appendix H De–Gaussifying the Gap: Linear Stationary Processes ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">H.6</span></a>, <a href="#A8.Thmtheorem4" title="Lemma H.4 (Moment expansions via cumulants). ‣ Cumulant identities for linear processes. ‣ Appendix H De–Gaussifying the Gap: Linear Stationary Processes ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">H.4</span></a> and <a href="#A8.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem H.5 (Order of the non–Gaussian gap). ‣ (d) Collecting the pieces. ‣ Appendix H De–Gaussifying the Gap: Linear Stationary Processes ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">H.5</span></a>).</span></span></span>
<span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.pic1.2.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.pic1.2.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Depth.<span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.pic1.2.4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Stacking layers enlarges the feature span, so risk is <em id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.pic1.2.4.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">monotone nonincreasing</em> in <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.pic1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>; generically it <em id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.pic1.2.4.1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">strictly</em> improves when the new layer adds a nonredundant direction (<a href="#A6.Thmtheorem18" title="Proposition F.18 (Monotone improvement with depth). ‣ Depth helps: a simple embedding argument. ‣ F.4 A finite–sample gap for 𝐿 stacked LSA layers (with monotone improvement) ‣ Appendix F Closed-Form Gap Characterization between LSA and Linear Models ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F.18</span></a>), yet the LR baseline remains unbeatable at finite <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.pic1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> (<a href="#A6.E12" title="In A one–shot convex relaxation for depth 𝐿. ‣ F.4 A finite–sample gap for 𝐿 stacked LSA layers (with monotone improvement) ‣ Appendix F Closed-Form Gap Characterization between LSA and Linear Models ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equations</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a> and <a href="#A6.E13" title="Equation 13 ‣ A one–shot convex relaxation for depth 𝐿. ‣ F.4 A finite–sample gap for 𝐿 stacked LSA layers (with monotone improvement) ‣ Appendix F Closed-Form Gap Characterization between LSA and Linear Models ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>).</span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Chain-of-Thought Rollout: Multi-Step Collapse</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we show that under CoT-style inference, LSA collapses to the mean with an error rate that grows exponentially. We first define CoT in <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem8" title="Definition 3.8 (Chain-of-Thought (CoT) Inference). ‣ 3.3 Chain-of-Thought Rollout: Multi-Step Collapse ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Definition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.8</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.8</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Chain-of-Thought (CoT) Inference)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem8.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Given a time series <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{1},\dots,x_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{1},\dots,x_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> and context length <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>, initialize the Hankel matrix <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{n}\in\mdmathbb{R}^{(p+1)\times(n-p+1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{n}\in\mdmathbb{R}^{(p+1)\times(n-p+1)}</annotation></semantics></math> as in <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem5" title="Definition 2.5 (Hankel Matrix). ‣ 2.3 In-Context Time Series Forecasting ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Definition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.5</span></a> with the last column zero-padded. Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\sf TF}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖳𝖥</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sf TF}</annotation></semantics></math> be the <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-layer LSA-based Transformer in <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem4" title="Definition 2.4 (𝐿-Layer LSA-Only Transformer). ‣ 2.2 Transformer Architecture ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Definition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a>. For each step <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=1,2,\dots,T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=1,2,\dots,T</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<ol id="S3.I1" class="ltx_enumerate ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Predict the next value:
</span><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\widehat{x}_{n+t}:=[{\sf TF}(H_{n+t-1})]_{(p+1,\,n-p+t)}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>:=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>𝖳𝖥</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\widehat{x}_{n+t}:=[{\sf TF}(H_{n+t-1})]_{(p+1,\,n-p+t)}.</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Overwrite the zero in the last column of </span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{n+t-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{n+t-1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> with </span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{x}_{n+t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{x}_{n+t}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Append the column </span><math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{n-p+t+1},\dots,x_{n},\widehat{x}_{n+1},\dots,\widehat{x}_{n+t})^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{n-p+t+1},\dots,x_{n},\widehat{x}_{n+1},\dots,\widehat{x}_{n+t})^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> to form </span><math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{n+t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{n+t}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> with last entry set to </span><math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Repeating yields CoT rollouts <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{x}_{n+1},\dots,\widehat{x}_{n+T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{x}_{n+1},\dots,\widehat{x}_{n+T}</annotation></semantics></math> by feeding model outputs back into the Hankel input.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.9</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Collapse and error compounding for CoT)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem9.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Under AR(<math id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>), the Bayes <math id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>-step forecast equals the noise-free recursive rollout of the one-step Bayes predictor. Any stable linear CoT recursion <math id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{s}_{t+1}=A(w)\widehat{s}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{s}_{t+1}=A(w)\widehat{s}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> collapses exponentially to <math id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>. For Bayes <math id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w=\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w=\rho</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathrm{MSE}^{*}(h)=\mdmathbb E[(x_{n+h}-\widehat{x}^{*}_{n+h})^{2}]=\sigma_{\varepsilon}^{2}\sum_{k=0}^{h-1}{}_{k}^{2}\nearrow\mathrm{Var}(x_{t}),\quad\mathrm{Var}(x_{t})-\mathrm{MSE}^{*}(h)\leq\tfrac{C^{2}\sigma_{\varepsilon}^{2}}{1-\beta^{2}}\beta^{2h}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>MSE</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">"</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0.111em">↗</mo><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn><mprescripts></mprescripts><mi>k</mi><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mi>Var</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mi>Var</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>MSE</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">"</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{MSE}^{*}(h)=\mdmathbb E[(x_{n+h}-\widehat{x}^{*}_{n+h})^{2}]=\sigma_{\varepsilon}^{2}\sum_{k=0}^{h-1}{}_{k}^{2}\nearrow\mathrm{Var}(x_{t}),\quad\mathrm{Var}(x_{t})-\mathrm{MSE}^{*}(h)\leq\tfrac{C^{2}\sigma_{\varepsilon}^{2}}{1-\beta^{2}}\beta^{2h}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Thus, even for the optimal predictor, CoT error compounds to the unconditional variance at an exponential rate governed by the spectrum of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A(\rho)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A(\rho)</annotation></semantics></math>. Here <math id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> are constants depending only on the AR(<math id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>) process.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Proof deferred to <a href="#A7" title="Appendix G Chain-of-Thought (CoT) Rollout in TSF: Collapse-to-Mean and Error Compounding ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">G</span></a>.</em>
Because conditional expectation is the <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> projection, for any measurable <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="g=g(x_{1:n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=g(x_{1:n})</annotation></semantics></math> (including any <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-layer LSA CoT rollout) one has</p>
<table id="S3.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mdmathbb E[(x_{n+h}-g)^{2}]=\mathrm{MSE}^{*}(h)+\mdmathbb E[(\widehat{x}^{*}_{n+h}-g)^{2}]\ \geq\ \mathrm{MSE}^{*}(h)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>MSE</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>−</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.778em">≥</mo><mrow><msup><mi>MSE</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mdmathbb E[(x_{n+h}-g)^{2}]=\mathrm{MSE}^{*}(h)+\mdmathbb E[(\widehat{x}^{*}_{n+h}-g)^{2}]\ \geq\ \mathrm{MSE}^{*}(h),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">with strictness unless <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\equiv\widehat{x}^{*}_{n+h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≡</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\equiv\widehat{x}^{*}_{n+h}</annotation></semantics></math> a.s. (<a href="#A7.E16" title="In LSA (or any alternative) is uniformly dominated at every horizon. ‣ Appendix G Chain-of-Thought (CoT) Rollout in TSF: Collapse-to-Mean and Error Compounding ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equations</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a> and <a href="#A7.E17" title="Equation 17 ‣ LSA (or any alternative) is uniformly dominated at every horizon. ‣ Appendix G Chain-of-Thought (CoT) Rollout in TSF: Collapse-to-Mean and Error Compounding ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a>). Since one-layer LSA already has a strict finite-sample gap at <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h=1</annotation></semantics></math> (<a href="#S3.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 3.4 (Strict finite-sample gap: AR(𝑝)). ‣ 3.2 A Strict Finite-Sample Gap (Core Result) ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>), equality fails generically for all <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 3.10</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Earlier failure of LSA CoT)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem10.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Define the failure horizon <math id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\tau}(g):=\inf\{h\geq 1:\mdmathbb E[(x_{n+h}-g_{h})^{2}]\geq\tau\,\mathrm{Var}(x_{t})\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">:=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">inf</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Var</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\tau}(g):=\inf\{h\geq 1:\mdmathbb E[(x_{n+h}-g_{h})^{2}]\geq\tau\,\mathrm{Var}(x_{t})\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\tau}(\widehat{x}^{\mathrm{LSA}})\leq H_{\tau}(\widehat{x}^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>LSA</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\tau}(\widehat{x}^{\mathrm{LSA}})\leq H_{\tau}(\widehat{x}^{*})</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>, with strict inequality on a set of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> of positive measure. In words: LSA CoT reaches the large-error regime no later than (and generically earlier than) Bayes linear regression.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Proof deferred to <a href="#A7" title="Appendix G Chain-of-Thought (CoT) Rollout in TSF: Collapse-to-Mean and Error Compounding ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">G</span></a>.</em>
Quantitatively, <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Var}(x_{t})-\mathrm{MSE}^{\mathrm{LSA}}(h)\leq\mathrm{Var}(x_{t})-\mathrm{MSE}^{*}(h)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Var</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>MSE</mi><mi>LSA</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Var</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>MSE</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Var}(x_{t})-\mathrm{MSE}^{\mathrm{LSA}}(h)\leq\mathrm{Var}(x_{t})-\mathrm{MSE}^{*}(h),</annotation></semantics></math>
so whenever the gap to variance remains positive, LSA’s CoT error approaches <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Var}(x_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Var</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Var}(x_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> at least exponentially fast; if it overshoots (the left side becomes negative), <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem10" title="Corollary 3.10 (Earlier failure of LSA CoT). ‣ 3.3 Chain-of-Thought Rollout: Multi-Step Collapse ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Corollary</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.10</span></a> still guarantees earlier threshold crossing. For AR(1), closed forms make the compounding explicit: <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{MSE}^{*}(h)=\sigma^{2}(1-\rho^{2h})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>MSE</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{MSE}^{*}(h)=\sigma^{2}(1-\rho^{2h})</annotation></semantics></math> with half-life <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{1/2}=\log(1/2)/\log(\rho^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{1/2}=\log(1/2)/\log(\rho^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S3.SS3.p4.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="201.7" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 650 201.7" width="650"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,201.7) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#CCCCFF;" fill="#CCCCFF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 9.26 L 0 192.44 C 0 197.55 4.14 201.7 9.26 201.7 L 640.74 201.7 C 645.86 201.7 650 197.55 650 192.44 L 650 9.26 C 650 4.14 645.86 0 640.74 0 L 9.26 0 C 4.14 0 0 4.14 0 9.26 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F5F5F5;" fill="#F5F5F5" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.38 9.26 L 1.38 178.76 L 648.62 178.76 L 648.62 9.26 C 648.62 4.91 645.09 1.38 640.74 1.38 L 9.26 1.38 C 4.91 1.38 1.38 4.91 1.38 9.26 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 9.26 186.77)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:39.69em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="12.3" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="549.2"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S3.SS3.p4.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:39.69em;">
<span id="S3.SS3.p4.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p4.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Takeaway 3: CoT Collapse in TSF</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 9.26 11.95)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:45.64em;--ltx-fo-height:11.49em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="161.63" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 158.94)" width="631.53"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S3.SS3.p4.pic1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:45.64em;">
<span id="S3.SS3.p4.pic1.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p4.pic1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">TSF.<span id="S3.SS3.p4.pic1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> CoT rollout forms a stable linear dynamical system that <em id="S3.SS3.p4.pic1.2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">collapses to the mean</em> and whose error <em id="S3.SS3.p4.pic1.2.1.1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">compounds exponentially</em> to <math id="S3.SS3.p4.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Var}(x_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">Var</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Var}(x_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>; Bayes/LR is horizonwise optimal and LSA CoT is uniformly dominated at each horizon (<a href="#A7.Thmtheorem1" title="Proposition G.1 (Bayes multi–step forecast equals recursive rollout). ‣ CoT rollout. ‣ Appendix G Chain-of-Thought (CoT) Rollout in TSF: Collapse-to-Mean and Error Compounding ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Propositions</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">G.1</span></a>, <a href="#A7.Thmtheorem2" title="Lemma G.2 (Exponential decay for any stable CoT). ‣ CoT rollout. ‣ Appendix G Chain-of-Thought (CoT) Rollout in TSF: Collapse-to-Mean and Error Compounding ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">G.2</span></a>, <a href="#A7.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem G.3 (CoT collapse and compounding error). ‣ Bayes multi–step error (ground truth model). ‣ Appendix G Chain-of-Thought (CoT) Rollout in TSF: Collapse-to-Mean and Error Compounding ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">G.3</span></a>, <a href="#A7.E17" title="Equation 17 ‣ LSA (or any alternative) is uniformly dominated at every horizon. ‣ Appendix G Chain-of-Thought (CoT) Rollout in TSF: Collapse-to-Mean and Error Compounding ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a> and <a href="#A7.Thmtheorem4" title="Corollary G.4 (Earlier threshold crossing for LSA). ‣ LSA (or any alternative) is uniformly dominated at every horizon. ‣ Appendix G Chain-of-Thought (CoT) Rollout in TSF: Collapse-to-Mean and Error Compounding ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">G.4</span></a>).</span></span></span>
<span id="S3.SS3.p4.pic1.2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p4.pic1.2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Contrast.<span id="S3.SS3.p4.pic1.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Notably, CoT behaves very differently in TSF compared to other domains: in language tasks, test-time scaling shows longer inference chains <em id="S3.SS3.p4.pic1.2.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">improve</em> problem solving <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">62</a>, <a href="#bib.bib89" title="" class="ltx_ref">89</a>]</cite>, and CoT can help in in-context linear regression <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite>; in stark contrast, in TSF, CoT leads to <em id="S3.SS3.p4.pic1.2.2.1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">rapidly compounding</em> forecast errors.</span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Numerical Verification</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We defer experimental details, including datasets, model configurations, and the Softmax attention vs. LSA comparison, to <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Experimental Details ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Evaluation</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To comprehensively assess model performance in TSF, we employ two complementary inference modes for evaluation and visualization:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S4.I1" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Teacher-Forcing (TF) TSF:</span>
This method evaluates the model under idealized conditions by providing ground-truth historical values as inputs at <em id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">each</em> time step. It is commonly used to measure predictive accuracy and to visualize the model’s capacity to fit the true data distribution.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Chain-of-Thought (CoT) TSF:</span>
This iterative inference approach simulates real-world deployment by using the model’s own past predictions as inputs for future steps. It enables the evaluation of long-horizon stability and the extent of error accumulation during rollout.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Evaluation Metrics.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{x_{1},x_{2},\dots,x_{T}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{x_{1},x_{2},\dots,x_{T}\}</annotation></semantics></math> denote a ground-truth test time series and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\widehat{x}_{1},\widehat{x}_{2},\dots,\widehat{x}_{T}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\widehat{x}_{1},\widehat{x}_{2},\dots,\widehat{x}_{T}\}</annotation></semantics></math> the corresponding model predictions.
We evaluate forecasting accuracy using the Mean Squared Error (MSE):
<math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{MSE}:=\frac{1}{T}\sum_{t=1}^{T}(x_{t}-\widehat{x}_{t})^{2}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>MSE</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>T</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{MSE}:=\frac{1}{T}\sum_{t=1}^{T}(x_{t}-\widehat{x}_{t})^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math>
In rollouts where predictions are generated via TF or CoT, we further compute the <em id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">cumulative MSE</em> up to step <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>:
<math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CMSE}(k):=\frac{1}{k}\sum_{t=1}^{k}(x_{t}-\widehat{x}_{t})^{2},k\in[T]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>CMSE</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>k</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CMSE}(k):=\frac{1}{k}\sum_{t=1}^{k}(x_{t}-\widehat{x}_{t})^{2},k\in[T].</annotation></semantics></math>
This metric reflects how prediction errors <em id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">accumulate over time</em> as inference unfolds, providing a smoother depiction of trajectory-level performance when pointwise errors are noisy or scattered <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib80" title="" class="ltx_ref">80</a>, <a href="#bib.bib96" title="" class="ltx_ref">96</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Experiments</h3>

<figure id="S4.F1" class="ltx_figure">
<table id="S4.F1.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S4.F1.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.F1.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.09776v1/tf_values_p5_L1.svg" id="S4.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:148/89;" width="148" height="89"></object></td>
<td id="S4.F1.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.09776v1/tf_cum_mse_p5_L1.svg" id="S4.F1.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:148/89;" width="148" height="89"></object></td>
<td id="S4.F1.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.09776v1/context_scaling_lsa_vs_ols_tf.svg" id="S4.F1.g3" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:131/91;" width="131" height="91"></object></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.F1.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.F1.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S4.F1.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">(a) TF Values</span></td>
<td id="S4.F1.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S4.F1.2.2.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">(c) TF Cumulative MSE</span></td>
<td id="S4.F1.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S4.F1.2.2.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">(e) Context Scaling</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.F1.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.F1.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.09776v1/cot_values_p5_L1.svg" id="S4.F1.g4" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:148/89;" width="148" height="89"></object></td>
<td id="S4.F1.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.09776v1/cot_cum_mse_p5_L1.svg" id="S4.F1.g5" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:148/89;" width="148" height="89"></object></td>
<td id="S4.F1.2.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.09776v1/lsa_layers_lsa_vs_ols_tf.svg" id="S4.F1.g6" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:131/89;" width="131" height="89"></object></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.F1.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.F1.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S4.F1.2.4.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">(b) CoT Values</span></td>
<td id="S4.F1.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S4.F1.2.4.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">(d) CoT Cumulative MSE</span></td>
<td id="S4.F1.2.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S4.F1.2.4.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">(f) LSA Layer Scaling</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Experimental results.
<span id="S4.F1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(a–b)</span> Predictions under Teacher-Forcing (TF) and Chain-of-Thought (CoT).
<span id="S4.F1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(c–d)</span> Cumulative MSE for TF and CoT rollouts.
<span id="S4.F1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(e–f)</span> Scaling experiments varying the history length and the number of LSA layers.
Overall, LSA tracks AR(<math id="S4.F1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>) but never surpasses the OLS baseline, confirming its representational limits.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For all experiments, we adopt a common set of hyperparameters.
Synthetic AR series are generated with Gaussian noise of zero mean and standard deviation <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\varepsilon}=0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">"</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\varepsilon}=0.05</annotation></semantics></math>
and total length <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="50{,}000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>50,000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">50{,}000</annotation></semantics></math>, split into training/validation/test with proportions <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.70/0.15/0.15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.70</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>0.15</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>0.15</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.70/0.15/0.15</annotation></semantics></math>.
Models are trained on 10 RTX 2080 GPUs for up to <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>100</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">100</annotation></semantics></math> epochs, using a batch size of <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="512" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>512</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">512</annotation></semantics></math>
and the Adam optimizer with learning rate <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{-3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{-3}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Teacher Forcing vs. Chain-of-Thought.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We compare <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>50</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">50</annotation></semantics></math>-step predictions under Teacher-Forcing (TF) and Chain-of-Thought (CoT) rollout (<a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Evaluation ‣ 4 Numerical Verification ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>) using a one-layer LSA with <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=8</annotation></semantics></math> on AR(<math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math>).
Figures <a href="#S4.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 4.2 Experiments ‣ 4 Numerical Verification ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>(a–b,c–d) show trajectories and cumulative MSEs.
Under TF, both LSA and OLS track the AR(<math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math>) process, but OLS consistently yields lower MSE, indicating LSA fits yet never exceeds the linear baseline.
Under CoT rollout, errors accumulate: both methods collapse to the mean, with LSA failing earlier, consistent with <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 Chain-of-Thought Rollout: Multi-Step Collapse ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Context and Layers Scaling.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We vary history length <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> and LSA layers <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in\{3,5,7\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in\{3,5,7\}</annotation></semantics></math>, averaged over 7 seeds with error bars indicating the standard error of the mean (SEM).
For context scaling, <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=p+\{5,25,50,100,200\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>25</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>50</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>100</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>200</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=p+\{5,25,50,100,200\}</annotation></semantics></math> with one LSA layer; for layer scaling, <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\in\{1,\dots,5\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\in\{1,\dots,5\}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=100</annotation></semantics></math>.
Figures <a href="#S4.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 4.2 Experiments ‣ 4 Numerical Verification ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>(e–f) show Teacher-Forcing MSE: larger <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> improves LSA but never closes the gap to OLS, and more layers give diminishing gains saturating at the OLS level, consistent with <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 A Strict Finite-Sample Gap (Core Result) ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Discussion</h2>

<section id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Architectural Considerations.</h5>

<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Beyond LSA, several components may influence Transformer performance.
The Softmax in standard attention may enhance expressivity by exponentiating inputs, effectively expanding the representation space and approximating an infinite series to capture richer dependencies (see experimental results in <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Experimental Details ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>).
Given the limitations of single-head LSA, simply aggregating multiple heads over the same data source is unlikely to improve expressivity; however, allocating different heads to distinct modalities may offer benefits through data fusion. Additionally, the role of feedforward MLP layers deserves closer scrutiny. Although not the focus of our analysis, prior work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib109" title="" class="ltx_ref">109</a>, <a href="#bib.bib91" title="" class="ltx_ref">91</a>, <a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>, <a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">70</a>]</cite> suggests that MLPs play as key contributors in time series tasks—potentially explaining the performance of LLMs in TSF. We leave these directions to future work.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Difference Between Language and Time Series.</h5>

<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Attention serves as a learned compression mechanism, essential in language modeling where meaning depends on long-range, abstract dependencies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bib90" title="" class="ltx_ref">90</a>, <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>, <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite>. In contrast, time series with low-order dynamics (e.g., AR(<math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>)) hinge on local, position-specific patterns, where such compression can obscure predictive signals. Because attention applies fixed contextual weights, it often fails to capture these direct dependencies, explaining the locality-agnostic behavior noted in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">59</a>]</cite>. When compression is misaligned with the data-generating process, classical models typically outperform attention.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We study the limits of Transformers in time-series forecasting via in-context learning theory. For autoregressive (AR) processes, we prove that Linear Self-Attention (LSA) cannot beat the optimal linear predictor, yielding a strictly positive gap in expectation.
Our analysis further shows that although LSA asymptotically recovers the linear predictor under teacher-forcing, errors compound under Chain-of-Thought rollout, ultimately causing collapse-to-mean behavior.
Experiments across teacher-forcing, CoT, and scaling corroborate our theory: LSA matches but never exceeds the linear baseline.
These findings clarify the inherent limits of attention in time-series forecasting and highlight the need for architectures beyond self-attention to capture temporal structure.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx1" class="ltx_section">
