<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:173%;">Appendix<span id="p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text"></span></span></p>
</div>
<section id="A0.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Roadmap.</h5>

<div id="A0.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A0.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A More Related Work ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>, we review related work on Transformers for time series forecasting, in-context learning theory, and representational limitations of attention.
<a href="#A2" title="Appendix B More Discussion ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a> provides further discussion on our perspective of TSF.
In <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Experimental Details ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>, we include more experiment details.
<a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Time Series Fundamentals ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a> reviews classical results on time series.
<a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Transformers Expressivity for TSF ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a> analyzes the expressivity of LSA Transformers for TSF.
<a href="#A6" title="Appendix F Closed-Form Gap Characterization between LSA and Linear Models ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F</span></a> establishes the finite-sample gap between LSA and linear models, with detailed proofs.
<a href="#A7" title="Appendix G Chain-of-Thought (CoT) Rollout in TSF: Collapse-to-Mean and Error Compounding ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">G</span></a> extends our analysis to Chain-of-Thought rollout, characterizing collapse-to-mean and error compounding.
Finally, <a href="#A8" title="Appendix H De–Gaussifying the Gap: Linear Stationary Processes ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">H</span></a> relaxes Gaussian assumptions and generalizes our results to linear stationary processes.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>More Related Work</h2>

<section id="A1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.1 </span>Transformers for Time Series Forecasting</h3>

<div id="A1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Early adaptations of Transformers for time series forecasting primarily modified attention mechanisms to capture long-term dependencies efficiently. Informer introduced ProbSparse attention to mitigate quadratic complexity <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib115" title="" class="ltx_ref">115</a>]</cite>, while Pyraformer employed hierarchical pyramidal attention for multi-scale modeling <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">68</a>]</cite>. Autoformer and FEDformer further integrated domain-specific inductive biases, utilizing auto-correlation and frequency decomposition, respectively, to model seasonal-trend components explicitly <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib101" title="" class="ltx_ref">101</a>, <a href="#bib.bib111" title="" class="ltx_ref">111</a>]</cite>. Additional variants include locality-enhanced attention <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">59</a>]</cite>, inverted architectures <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">57</a>]</cite>, and tokenization-based representations treating sequences as textual patches <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib78" title="" class="ltx_ref">78</a>]</cite>. A comprehensive overview is presented in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib102" title="" class="ltx_ref">102</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">More recent studies leverage pretrained Large Language Models (LLMs) to transfer NLP-style capabilities to forecasting tasks. Zero-shot forecasting was initially demonstrated using pretrained LLMs without task-specific tuning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>. Subsequent works explored specialized prompt-based strategies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bib79" title="" class="ltx_ref">79</a>]</cite>, reprogramming pretrained LLMs directly <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>]</cite>, and unified, dataset-agnostic training paradigms <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib99" title="" class="ltx_ref">99</a>, <a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">66</a>]</cite>. Broader frameworks proposed foundational-model perspectives for time series tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>, discrete vocabulary tokenization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>, multi-patch prediction <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>, and generalized decoder-only architectures <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib112" title="" class="ltx_ref">112</a>, <a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">63</a>]</cite>. Recent surveys systematically summarize these emerging paradigms and highlight open challenges and opportunities in deploying LLMs for time series analysis <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib108" title="" class="ltx_ref">108</a>, <a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">67</a>, <a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">60</a>, <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.2 </span>In-Context Learning Theory</h3>

<div id="A1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recent work theoretically interprets in-context learning (ICL) as a Transformer forward pass implicitly performing variants of gradient descent (GD). Early studies empirically demonstrated Transformers can closely approximate ordinary-least-squares predictors <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>, while subsequent constructive analyses showed one linear self-attention (LSA) layer corresponds exactly to one GD step, with the global training objective implementing a preconditioned, Bayes-optimal GD step <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib93" title="" class="ltx_ref">93</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">73</a>]</cite>. Further training-dynamics analyses establish gradient flow convergence of LSA to learn the class of linear models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib110" title="" class="ltx_ref">110</a>]</cite>, provide finite-time convergence guarantees and parameter evolution for multi-head Softmax attention <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>, <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite>, and identify phase transitions revealing when linear-attention mimics full Transformer behaviors <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib113" title="" class="ltx_ref">113</a>]</cite>. Extensions include multi-step GD via chain-of-thought prompting <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite> and kernelized polynomial regression through gated linear units <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib88" title="" class="ltx_ref">88</a>]</cite>. Other works establish positive approximation guarantees for ICL in dynamical and autoregressive settings but lack universal lower bounds or explicit representational constraints <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">58</a>, <a href="#bib.bib85" title="" class="ltx_ref">85</a>, <a href="#bib.bib97" title="" class="ltx_ref">97</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.3 </span>Representational Limitations of Transformers</h3>

<div id="A1.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Despite their success, Transformers exhibit fundamental limitations in expressivity. Pure self-attention without MLPs suffers doubly exponential rank collapse with depth, severely constraining representational capacity <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>. Self-attention cannot model periodic finite-state languages unless the depth or number of heads scales with input length <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>. Complexity analyses show that log-precision Transformers are no more powerful than <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{TC}^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝖳𝖢</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{TC}^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> circuits, implying provable failure on linear systems and context-free languages under standard complexity separations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib76" title="" class="ltx_ref">76</a>]</cite>.
Communication-complexity arguments further reveal that Transformers cannot compose functions over sufficiently large input domains <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib81" title="" class="ltx_ref">81</a>]</cite>, and their performance is task-dependent, achieving logarithmic complexity in input size for sparse averaging tasks, but requiring linear complexity for triple-detection <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib86" title="" class="ltx_ref">86</a>]</cite>.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> establish unconditional depth–width trade-offs, proving that solving sequential <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-step function composition tasks over input of <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> tokens requires either <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">
</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega(L)</annotation></semantics></math> layers or <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="n^{\Omega(1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">
</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n^{\Omega(1)}</annotation></semantics></math> hidden dimensions. Empirically, Transformers struggle with compositional generalization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> and fail to outperform RNNs in modeling Hidden Markov dynamics <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite>. While chain-of-thought prompting and positional embeddings can recover arithmetic and step-wise reasoning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib71" title="" class="ltx_ref">71</a>]</cite>, these function as external aids, underscoring the architecture’s inherent limitations.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>More Discussion</h2>

<section id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Other Data Distributions.</h5>

<div id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Although our analysis focuses on AR processes, similar considerations apply more broadly. The difficulty for attention arises from its misalignment in capturing input dependencies, as seen in AR(<math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>). We further discuss Moving Average (MA) processes and, more generally, ARMA models in <a href="#A8" title="Appendix H De–Gaussifying the Gap: Linear Stationary Processes ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">H</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Multivariate Time Series.</h5>

<div id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the case of uncorrelated multivariate time series, each dimension evolves independently, reducing training to separate LSA models.
This eliminates the opportunity to exploit cross-variable dependencies and limits the potential of learning shared structure.
Consequently, pretraining on a collection of uncorrelated time series may fail to produce useful shared representations.
For the correlated case, one could impose structural assumptions on inter-variable dependencies to enable tractable analysis. We leave the investigation of such multivariate models, such as Vector Auto-Regression (VAR) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>, to future work.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Optimization Dynamics.</h5>

<div id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While our analysis primarily addresses representational limitations of attention mechanisms, future work could explore optimization dynamics and training difficulties of Transformers for time series forecasting. Understanding these issues might yield complementary insights into observed empirical shortcomings.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Real-World Complexities.</h5>

<div id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Real-world forecasting tasks include many complexities not modeled in this study, such as data randomness, intricate temporal dependencies, training instability, noisy signals, and external factors like market sentiment <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">67</a>, <a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">60</a>]</cite>. Exploring these practical challenges could help bridge theoretical findings with real-world performance.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Architectural and Framework Varieties.</h5>

<div id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our framework intentionally abstracts away practical components such as Rotary Position Embeddings (RoPE) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib84" title="" class="ltx_ref">84</a>]</cite> and Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>. Future work may assess whether these enhancements alleviate the representational limitations we identify. State Space Models like Mamba <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> also present a promising alternative for time series forecasting <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib98" title="" class="ltx_ref">98</a>]</cite>. Beyond architectural changes, generative modeling paradigms such as diffusion models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite> and flow matching <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">55</a>]</cite> offer alternative approaches for time series <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib92" title="" class="ltx_ref">92</a>, <a href="#bib.bib106" title="" class="ltx_ref">106</a>, <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>, <a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">69</a>, <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, <a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">65</a>]</cite>, potentially overcoming the limitations of Transformer-based Next-Token Prediction objectives.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Experimental Details</h2>

<div id="A3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section provides additional details for <a href="#S4" title="4 Numerical Verification ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="A3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.1 </span>Dataset and Model Configuration</h3>

<section id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Synthetic data.</h5>

<div id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We generate <em id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">stable</em> AR(<math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>) processes (<a href="#S2.Thmtheorem2" title="Definition 2.2 (AR(𝑝) Process []). ‣ 2.1 Time Series ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Definition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>) by sampling coefficient vectors (roots outside the unit circle), adding Gaussian noise, discarding a short burn-in, and retaining a long sequence. Each sequence is split into train/validation/test segments.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">From long sequences to training examples.</h5>

<div id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We fix a history length <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n&gt;p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n&gt;p</annotation></semantics></math>. For each series <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="x_{1:T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1:T}</annotation></semantics></math>, a sliding window of length <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> with stride 1 defines training pairs with input history <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="x_{t-n+1:t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t-n+1:t}</annotation></semantics></math> and target <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t+1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Each history is transformed into a Hankel matrix <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{n}^{(t)}\in\mdmathbb{R}^{(p+1)\times(n-p+1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{n}^{(t)}\in\mdmathbb{R}^{(p+1)\times(n-p+1)}</annotation></semantics></math> (<a href="#S2.Thmtheorem5" title="Definition 2.5 (Hankel Matrix). ‣ 2.3 In-Context Time Series Forecasting ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Definition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.5</span></a>), which serves as the input to the LSA model.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Models.</h5>

<div id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our main model is an <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-layer LSA-only Transformer <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\sf TF}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖳𝖥</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sf TF}</annotation></semantics></math> (<a href="#S2.Thmtheorem4" title="Definition 2.4 (𝐿-Layer LSA-Only Transformer). ‣ 2.2 Transformer Architecture ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Definition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a>) with feature dimension <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=p</annotation></semantics></math>. We read prediction from the label slot:
<math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{x}_{t+1}=\bigl[{\sf TF}(H_{n}^{(t)})\bigr]_{(p+1,n-p+1)}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><mi>𝖳𝖥</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{x}_{t+1}=\bigl[{\sf TF}(H_{n}^{(t)})\bigr]_{(p+1,n-p+1)}.</annotation></semantics></math>
As a baseline, we fit a classical AR(<math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>) predictor by OLS on the same training series used for LSA.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Training.</h5>

<div id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All windows are shuffled and batched. Models are trained with teacher forcing using MSE loss and Adam. We sweep <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>, while keeping the noise level and optimization hyperparameters fixed. Performance is reported on the held-out test split.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.2 </span>Softmax Attention vs. LSA</h3>

<figure id="A3.F2" class="ltx_figure">
<table id="A3.F2.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="A3.F2.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A3.F2.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.09776v1/softmax_tf_values_p5_L1.svg" id="A3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:215/129;" width="215" height="129"></object></td>
<td id="A3.F2.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.09776v1/softmax_tf_cum_mse_p5_L1.svg" id="A3.F2.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:215/129;" width="215" height="129"></object></td></tr>
<tr id="A3.F2.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A3.F2.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="A3.F2.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">(a) TF Values</span></td>
<td id="A3.F2.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="A3.F2.2.2.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">(c) TF Cumulative MSE</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A3.F2.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A3.F2.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.09776v1/softmax_cot_values_p5_L1.svg" id="A3.F2.g3" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:215/129;" width="215" height="129"></object></td>
<td id="A3.F2.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.09776v1/softmax_cot_cum_mse_p5_L1.svg" id="A3.F2.g4" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:215/129;" width="215" height="129"></object></td></tr>
<tr id="A3.F2.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A3.F2.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="A3.F2.2.4.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">(b) CoT Values</span></td>
<td id="A3.F2.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="A3.F2.2.4.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">(d) CoT Cumulative MSE</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Experimental results on comparison of LSA and Softmax Attention.
<span id="A3.F2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(a–b)</span> Predictions under Teacher-Forcing (TF) and Chain-of-Thought (CoT).
<span id="A3.F2.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(c–d)</span> Cumulative MSE for TF and CoT rollouts.
Overall, both LSA and Softmax Attention tracks AR(<math id="A3.F2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>) but never surpass the OLS baseline. Moreover, Softmax Attention is slightly better than LSA.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Though theoretically more challenging, we could examine the experimental behavioral comparison between Softmax attention and LSA. We define Softmax attention as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A3.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition C.1</span></span><span id="A3.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Softmax Attention)<span id="A3.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A3.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="A3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H\in\mdmathbb{R}^{(d+1)\times(m+1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H\in\mdmathbb{R}^{(d+1)\times(m+1)}</annotation></semantics></math> be the input matrix and define the causal mask</span></p>
<table id="A3.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M:=\begin{bmatrix}I_{m}&amp;0\\
0&amp;0\end{bmatrix}\in\mdmathbb{R}^{(m+1)\times(m+1)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M:=\begin{bmatrix}I_{m}&amp;0\\
0&amp;0\end{bmatrix}\in\mdmathbb{R}^{(m+1)\times(m+1)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We denote the reparameterized weights <math id="A3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P,Q\in\mdmathbb{R}^{(d+1)\times(d+1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P,Q\in\mdmathbb{R}^{(d+1)\times(d+1)}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then the (masked) softmax attention output is defined as</span></p>
<table id="A3.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\sf Attn}(H):=PHM\,{\sf Softmax}\bigl(H^{\top}QH\bigr)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝖠𝗍𝗍𝗇</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖲𝗈𝖿𝗍𝗆𝖺𝗑</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sf Attn}(H):=PHM\,{\sf Softmax}\bigl(H^{\top}QH\bigr),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.Thmtheorem1.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.Thmtheorem1.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where the <math id="A3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\sf Softmax}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖲𝗈𝖿𝗍𝗆𝖺𝗑</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sf Softmax}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is applied column-wise to ensure attention weights are normalized.
Thus <math id="A3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\sf Attn}(H)\in\mdmathbb{R}^{(d+1)\times(m+1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝖠𝗍𝗍𝗇</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sf Attn}(H)\in\mdmathbb{R}^{(d+1)\times(m+1)}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We compare 1-layer LSA and softmax attention models under identical settings (Adam optimizer and hyperparameters as in <a href="#S4" title="4 Numerical Verification ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>), differing only in architecture. Empirically, Softmax Attention performs slightly better than LSA (<a href="#A3.F2" title="In C.2 Softmax Attention vs. LSA ‣ Appendix C Experimental Details ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>), which is unsurprising given the greater expressivity underlying the strong performance of Transformers. However, the analytical complexity of the Softmax operator makes theoretical understanding more challenging, and we leave a deeper investigation of this gap to future work.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A4" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix D </span>Time Series Fundamentals</h2>

<div id="A4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We first recall classical results for stationary autoregressive (AR) processes, which form the theoretical backbone for our later analysis. These results connect population-level covariances to model coefficients, show how consistent estimates can be obtained from finite data, and establish a natural linear performance baseline.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A4.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition D.1</span></span><span id="A4.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Yule–Walker Equations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>)<span id="A4.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A4.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="A4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{x_{i}\}_{i=1}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{x_{i}\}_{i=1}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> follow a stationary AR(<math id="A4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>) process as in Definition <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem2" title="Definition 2.2 (AR(𝑝) Process []). ‣ 2.1 Time Series ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>. Define the Toeplitz autocovariance matrix <math id="A4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{p}\in\mdmathbb{R}^{p\times p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{p}\in\mdmathbb{R}^{p\times p}</annotation></semantics></math> as</span></p>
<table id="A4.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{p}:=\begin{pmatrix}{}_{0}&{}_{1}&amp;\cdots&{}_{p-1}\\
{}_{1}&{}_{0}&amp;\cdots&{}_{p-2}\\
\vdots&amp;\vdots&amp;\ddots&amp;\vdots\\
{}_{p-1}&{}_{p-2}&amp;\cdots&{}_{0}\end{pmatrix},\quad\gamma:=\begin{pmatrix}{}_{1}\\
{}_{2}\\
\vdots\\
{}_{p}\end{pmatrix}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mtd><mtd><mo>⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub></mtd><mtd><mo>⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd><mi mathvariant="normal">⋱</mi></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><mo>⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">
</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{p}:=\begin{pmatrix}{}_{0}&amp;{}_{1}&amp;\cdots&amp;{}_{p-1}\\
{}_{1}&amp;{}_{0}&amp;\cdots&amp;{}_{p-2}\\
\vdots&amp;\vdots&amp;\ddots&amp;\vdots\\
{}_{p-1}&amp;{}_{p-2}&amp;\cdots&amp;{}_{0}\end{pmatrix},\quad\gamma:=\begin{pmatrix}{}_{1}\\
{}_{2}\\
\vdots\\
{}_{p}\end{pmatrix}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Then the AR coefficient vector <math id="A4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho:=(\rho_{1},\ldots,\rho_{p})^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mo>:=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho:=(\rho_{1},\ldots,\rho_{p})^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies the Yule–Walker system:</span></p>
<table id="A4.Ex19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{p}\rho=\gamma." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">
</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{p}\rho=\gamma.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.Thmtheorem1.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.Thmtheorem1.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">This moment-matching condition links the autocovariance structure to the autoregressive coefficients.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A4.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A4.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem D.2</span></span><span id="A4.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(OLS Consistency for AR(<math id="A4.Thmtheorem2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>) Estimation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>)<span id="A4.Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A4.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="A4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{x_{i}\}_{i=1}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{x_{i}\}_{i=1}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> be generated by an AR(<math id="A4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>) process as in Definition <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem2" title="Definition 2.2 (AR(𝑝) Process []). ‣ 2.1 Time Series ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>. Let <math id="A4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\rho}_{n}:=(X^{\top}X)^{-1}X^{\top}y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\rho}_{n}:=(X^{\top}X)^{-1}X^{\top}y</annotation></semantics></math> be the ordinary least squares estimator obtained from</span></p>
<table id="A4.Ex20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y:=(x_{p+1},\dots,x_{n})^{\top},\quad X:=\begin{pmatrix}x_{p}&amp;x_{p-1}&amp;\cdots&amp;x_{1}\\
x_{p+1}&amp;x_{p}&amp;\cdots&amp;x_{2}\\
\vdots&amp;\vdots&amp;\ddots&amp;\vdots\\
x_{n-1}&amp;x_{n-2}&amp;\cdots&amp;x_{n-p}\end{pmatrix}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>:=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><mo>⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mtd><mtd><mo>⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd><mi mathvariant="normal">⋱</mi></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><mo>⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y:=(x_{p+1},\dots,x_{n})^{\top},\quad X:=\begin{pmatrix}x_{p}&amp;x_{p-1}&amp;\cdots&amp;x_{1}\\
x_{p+1}&amp;x_{p}&amp;\cdots&amp;x_{2}\\
\vdots&amp;\vdots&amp;\ddots&amp;\vdots\\
x_{n-1}&amp;x_{n-2}&amp;\cdots&amp;x_{n-p}\end{pmatrix}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Then, as <math id="A4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="A4.Ex21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\rho}_{n}\xrightarrow{\mathrm{a.s.}}\rho." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">a</mi><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="0.700em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">s</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="0.700em">.</mo></mrow></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\rho}_{n}\xrightarrow{\mathrm{a.s.}}\rho.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.Thmtheorem2.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.Thmtheorem2.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Hence, in the large-sample limit, OLS recovers the Bayes-optimal linear predictor in mean squared error.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A4.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A4.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem D.3</span></span><span id="A4.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Linear Baseline under AR(<math id="A4.Thmtheorem3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>) Dynamics)<span id="A4.Thmtheorem3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A4.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="A4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{x_{i}\}_{i=1}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{x_{i}\}_{i=1}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> be generated by an AR(<math id="A4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>) process as in Definition <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem2" title="Definition 2.2 (AR(𝑝) Process []). ‣ 2.1 Time Series ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>.
Fix a context window <math id="A4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="x_{1:n}\in\mdmathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>R</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1:n}\in\mdmathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq p</annotation></semantics></math>.
For any linear predictor <math id="A4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\widehat{x}_{n+1}^{\mathrm{LR}}=w^{\top}x_{1:n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>LR</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>w</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{x}_{n+1}^{\mathrm{LR}}=w^{\top}x_{1:n}</annotation></semantics></math> we have</span></p>
<table id="A4.Ex22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\min_{w\in\mdmathbb{R}^{n}}\mdmathbb{E}\left[(w^{\top}x_{1:n}-x_{n+1})^{2}\right]\;&lt;\;\mathrm{Var}(x_{n+1})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></munder><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo rspace="0.280em">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">&lt;</mo><mi>Var</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min_{w\in\mdmathbb{R}^{n}}\mdmathbb{E}\left[(w^{\top}x_{1:n}-x_{n+1})^{2}\right]\;&lt;\;\mathrm{Var}(x_{n+1}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.Thmtheorem3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.Thmtheorem3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where the expectation is over the stationary joint distribution of <math id="A4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{1},\dots,x_{n+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{1},\dots,x_{n+1})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A4.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">By Definition <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem2" title="Definition 2.2 (AR(𝑝) Process []). ‣ 2.1 Time Series ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>, <math id="A4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mdmathbb{E}[x_{i}]=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mdmathbb{E}[x_{i}]=0</annotation></semantics></math> and the noise variance is <math id="A4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The Bayes-optimal (and hence optimal linear) predictor is</span></p>
<table id="A4.Ex23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\widehat{x}_{n+1}^{\mathrm{LR}}=\mdmathbb{E}[x_{n+1}\mid x_{1:n}]=\sum_{j=1}^{p}\rho_{j}x_{n-j+1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>LR</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>p</mi></munderover><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{x}_{n+1}^{\mathrm{LR}}=\mdmathbb{E}[x_{n+1}\mid x_{1:n}]=\sum_{j=1}^{p}\rho_{j}x_{n-j+1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">Its mean squared error is</span></p>
<table id="A4.Ex24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.Ex24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mdmathbb{E}\left[\left(\widehat{x}_{n+1}^{\mathrm{LR}}-x_{n+1}\right)^{2}\right]=\sigma^{2}\;&lt;\;\sigma^{2}+\mathrm{Var}(x_{n})=\mathrm{Var}(x_{n+1})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>LR</mi></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo rspace="0.558em">&lt;</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>Var</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Var</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mdmathbb{E}\left[\left(\widehat{x}_{n+1}^{\mathrm{LR}}-x_{n+1}\right)^{2}\right]=\sigma^{2}\;&lt;\;\sigma^{2}+\mathrm{Var}(x_{n})=\mathrm{Var}(x_{n+1}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.p2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.p2.3.1" class="ltx_text">which is strictly below the variance baseline.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix E </span>Transformers Expressivity for TSF</h2>

<section id="A5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.1 </span>Fundamentals of Linear Self-Attention</h3>

<div id="A5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We work with Hankelized inputs for time-series forecasting. For context length <math id="A5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> and total length <math id="A5.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq p</annotation></semantics></math>, define the Hankel matrix</p>
<table id="A5.Ex25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H\;=\;\bigl[\,x^{(1)}\;x^{(2)}\;\dots\;x^{(n-p+1)}\bigr]\in\mdmathbb{R}^{(p+1)\times(n-p+1)}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.170em">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0.280em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H\;=\;\bigl[\,x^{(1)}\;x^{(2)}\;\dots\;x^{(n-p+1)}\bigr]\in\mdmathbb{R}^{(p+1)\times(n-p+1)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where each <math id="A5.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{(i)}\in\mdmathbb{R}^{p+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{(i)}\in\mdmathbb{R}^{p+1}</annotation></semantics></math> stacks a length-<math id="A5.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> window and a <math id="A5.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p+1)</annotation></semantics></math>-st <em id="A5.SS1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">label slot</em>; we reserve the last column <math id="A5.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{(n-p+1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{(n-p+1)}</annotation></semantics></math> as the prediction token (see <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem5" title="Definition 2.5 (Hankel Matrix). ‣ 2.3 In-Context Time Series Forecasting ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Definition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.5</span></a> for construction details).</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A5.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition E.1</span></span><span id="A5.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Linear Self-Attention (single layer))<span id="A5.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A5.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="A5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P,Q\in\mdmathbb{R}^{(p+1)\times(p+1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P,Q\in\mdmathbb{R}^{(p+1)\times(p+1)}</annotation></semantics></math> be trainable. Define</span></p>
<table id="A5.Ex26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\;:=\;H^{\top}QH\in\mdmathbb{R}^{(n-p+1)\times(n-p+1)},\qquad M\;:=\;\mathrm{diag}\bigl(I_{\,n-p},\,0\bigr)\in\mdmathbb{R}^{(n-p+1)\times(n-p+1)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">:=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">:=</mo><mrow><mi>diag</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mn> 0</mn><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\;:=\;H^{\top}QH\in\mdmathbb{R}^{(n-p+1)\times(n-p+1)},\qquad M\;:=\;\mathrm{diag}\bigl(I_{\,n-p},\,0\bigr)\in\mdmathbb{R}^{(n-p+1)\times(n-p+1)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The one-layer LSA update is</span></p>
<table id="A5.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\sf LSA}(H)\;:=\;H\;+\;\frac{1}{n}\;P\,\bigl(H\,M\,S\bigr)\;\in\;\mdmathbb{R}^{(p+1)\times(n-p+1)}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝖫𝖲𝖠</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">:=</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.502em" rspace="0.502em">+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0.280em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.280em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">∈</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sf LSA}(H)\;:=\;H\;+\;\frac{1}{n}\;P\,\bigl(H\,M\,S\bigr)\;\in\;\mdmathbb{R}^{(p+1)\times(n-p+1)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.Thmtheorem1.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.Thmtheorem1.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="A5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> masks the prediction token to avoid self-use during the update.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The multi-layer variant is defined by composing <math id="A5.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> residual bilinear updates.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A5.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition E.2</span></span><span id="A5.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<math id="A5.Thmtheorem2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-layer LSA Transformer)<span id="A5.Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A5.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For parameters <math id="A5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{P_{\ell},Q_{\ell}\}_{\ell=1}^{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{P_{\ell},Q_{\ell}\}_{\ell=1}^{L}</annotation></semantics></math>, define each layer as</span></p>
<table id="A5.Ex27" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\sf LSA}_{\ell}(H)\;:=\;H\;+\;\frac{1}{n}\,P_{\ell}\bigl(H\,M\,(H^{\top}Q_{\ell}H)\bigr)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝖫𝖲𝖠</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">:=</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.502em" rspace="0.502em">+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sf LSA}_{\ell}(H)\;:=\;H\;+\;\frac{1}{n}\,P_{\ell}\bigl(H\,M\,(H^{\top}Q_{\ell}H)\bigr),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and the overall <math id="A5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-layer LSA Transformer as</span></p>
<table id="A5.Ex28" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex28.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\sf TF}(H)\;:=\;{\sf LSA}_{L}\circ\cdots\circ{\sf LSA}_{1}(H)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖳𝖥</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">:=</mo><msub><mi>𝖫𝖲𝖠</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>𝖫𝖲𝖠</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sf TF}(H)\;:=\;{\sf LSA}_{L}\circ\cdots\circ{\sf LSA}_{1}(H).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We now formalize the readout at the prediction slot for a single LSA layer, which will later serve as the basic building block in our theoritical analysis.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A5.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma E.3</span></span><span id="A5.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Closed-form readout of one-layer LSA on Hankel input <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>)<span id="A5.Thmtheorem3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A5.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Following the construction in <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem5" title="Definition 2.5 (Hankel Matrix). ‣ 2.3 In-Context Time Series Forecasting ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Definition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.5</span></a> and <a href="#A5.Thmtheorem1" title="Definition E.1 (Linear Self-Attention (single layer)). ‣ E.1 Fundamentals of Linear Self-Attention ‣ Appendix E Transformers Expressivity for TSF ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Definition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.1</span></a>, the final column of <math id="A5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is the prediction token,</span></p>
<table id="A5.Ex29" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex29.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="x^{(n-p+1)}\;=\;\begin{bmatrix}x_{\,n-p+1:n}\\[2.0pt]
0\end{bmatrix},\qquad x_{\,n-p+1:n}\in\mdmathbb{R}^{p}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>R</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{(n-p+1)}\;=\;\begin{bmatrix}x_{\,n-p+1:n}\\[2.0pt]
0\end{bmatrix},\qquad x_{\,n-p+1:n}\in\mdmathbb{R}^{p}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.Thmtheorem3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.Thmtheorem3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose</span></p>
<table id="A5.Ex30" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\;=\;\begin{bmatrix}\mathbf{0}_{p\times(p+1)}\\[2.0pt]
b^{\top}\end{bmatrix},\qquad Q\;=\;\bigl[\,A\;\;\mathbf{0}_{(p+1)\times 1}\,\bigr]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.170em">[</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mn class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">  0</mn><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\;=\;\begin{bmatrix}\mathbf{0}_{p\times(p+1)}\\[2.0pt]
b^{\top}\end{bmatrix},\qquad Q\;=\;\bigl[\,A\;\;\mathbf{0}_{(p+1)\times 1}\,\bigr],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.Thmtheorem3.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.Thmtheorem3.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">with <math id="A5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\in\mdmathbb{R}^{p+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\in\mdmathbb{R}^{p+1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\in\mdmathbb{R}^{(p+1)\times p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\in\mdmathbb{R}^{(p+1)\times p}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the prediction-slot entry satisfies</span></p>
<table id="A5.Ex31" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex31.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\bigl[{\sf LSA}(H)\bigr]_{\,p+1,\,n-p+1}\;=\;b^{\top}\,G_{n}\,A\,x_{\,n-p+1:n}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mi>𝖫𝖲𝖠</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>A</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigl[{\sf LSA}(H)\bigr]_{\,p+1,\,n-p+1}\;=\;b^{\top}\,G_{n}\,A\,x_{\,n-p+1:n},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.Thmtheorem3.p1.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.Thmtheorem3.p1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where we define the empirical Gram matrix</span></p>
<table id="A5.Ex32" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{n}\;:=\;\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{\,n-p}x^{(i)}x^{(i)\top}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">:=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></munderover><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em">⊤</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{n}\;:=\;\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{\,n-p}x^{(i)}x^{(i)\top}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A5.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="A5.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=H^{\top}QH" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=H^{\top}QH</annotation></semantics></math>. Since the last column of <math id="A5.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is zero,</span></p>
<table id="A5.Ex33" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="HM=[\,x^{(1)}\;\dots\;x^{(n-p)}\;0\,]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0.280em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn> 0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">HM=[\,x^{(1)}\;\dots\;x^{(n-p)}\;0\,].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.SS1.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text">Because <math id="A5.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[H]_{p+1,\,n-p+1}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[H]_{p+1,\,n-p+1}=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A5.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{p+1}^{\top}P=b^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{p+1}^{\top}P=b^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="A5.Ex34" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigl[{\sf LSA}(H)\bigr]_{\,p+1,\,n-p+1}=\frac{1}{n}\,b^{\top}(HMS)e_{\,n-p+1}=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{j=1}^{\,n-p}b^{\top}x^{(j)}\,S_{j,\,n-p+1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><mi>𝖫𝖲𝖠</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></munderover><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigl[{\sf LSA}(H)\bigr]_{\,p+1,\,n-p+1}=\frac{1}{n}\,b^{\top}(HMS)e_{\,n-p+1}=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{j=1}^{\,n-p}b^{\top}x^{(j)}\,S_{j,\,n-p+1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS1.p4.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.SS1.p4.3.1" class="ltx_text">For <math id="A5.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\leq n-p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\leq n-p</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="A5.Ex35" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex35.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="S_{j,\,n-p+1}=x^{(j)\top}Qx^{(n-p+1)}=x^{(j)\top}A\,x_{\,n-p+1:n}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em">⊤</mo></mrow></msup><mi>Q</mi><mi>x</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em">⊤</mo></mrow></msup><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{j,\,n-p+1}=x^{(j)\top}Qx^{(n-p+1)}=x^{(j)\top}A\,x_{\,n-p+1:n},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS1.p4.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.SS1.p4.4.1" class="ltx_text">which gives the stated expression.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.2 </span>Function Space Constraints of Linear Self-Attention</h3>

<div id="A5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this subsection we take a function-space perspective on linear self-attention.
Rather than analyzing specific computations, we characterize the class of functions
that a one-layer LSA readout can realize on Hankelized inputs.
This higher-level view makes explicit that LSA predictions are confined to a
restricted polynomial feature space, and thus—despite the nontrivial attention
mechanism—cannot achieve fundamentally lower risk than classical linear regression
on AR(<math id="A5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>) processes.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A5.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma E.4</span></span><span id="A5.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Doob–Dynkin <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>]</cite>)<span id="A5.Thmtheorem4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A5.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="A5.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\Omega\to S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">
</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\Omega\to S</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A5.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g:\Omega\to T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">
</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g:\Omega\to T</annotation></semantics></math> be measurable mappings between measurable spaces. Then <math id="A5.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="A5.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(g)</annotation></semantics></math>-measurable if and only if there exists a measurable <math id="A5.Thmtheorem4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h:T\to S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h:T\to S</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="A5.Thmtheorem4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=h\circ g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=h\circ g</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A5.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A5.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem E.5</span></span><span id="A5.Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Projection Monotonicity in <math id="A5.Thmtheorem5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>)<span id="A5.Thmtheorem5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A5.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="A5.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> be a Hilbert space and <math id="A5.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{2}\subseteq M_{1}\subseteq H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>⊆</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊆</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{2}\subseteq M_{1}\subseteq H</annotation></semantics></math> be closed subspaces. For any <math id="A5.Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\in H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\in H</annotation></semantics></math>, letting <math id="A5.Thmtheorem5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{M_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{M_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the orthogonal projection onto <math id="A5.Thmtheorem5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</span></p>
<table id="A5.Ex36" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex36.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|Z-P_{M_{1}}Z\|\;\leq\;\|Z-P_{M_{2}}Z\|." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">≤</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|Z-P_{M_{1}}Z\|\;\leq\;\|Z-P_{M_{2}}Z\|.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<section id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Functional form of the one-layer readout.</h5>

<div id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H=[\,x^{(1)}\ \dots\ x^{(n-p+1)}\,]\in\mdmathbb{R}^{(p+1)\times(n-p+1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H=[\,x^{(1)}\ \dots\ x^{(n-p+1)}\,]\in\mdmathbb{R}^{(p+1)\times(n-p+1)}</annotation></semantics></math> be the Hankel matrix from <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem5" title="Definition 2.5 (Hankel Matrix). ‣ 2.3 In-Context Time Series Forecasting ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Definition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.5</span></a>.
Following <a href="#A5.Thmtheorem1" title="Definition E.1 (Linear Self-Attention (single layer)). ‣ E.1 Fundamentals of Linear Self-Attention ‣ Appendix E Transformers Expressivity for TSF ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Definition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.1</span></a>, the one-layer LSA update is</p>
<table id="A5.Ex37" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex37.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\sf LSA}(H)\;=\;H\;+\;\frac{1}{n}\,P\bigl(H\,M\,(H^{\top}QH)\bigr)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝖫𝖲𝖠</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.502em" rspace="0.502em">+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sf LSA}(H)\;=\;H\;+\;\frac{1}{n}\,P\bigl(H\,M\,(H^{\top}QH)\bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Following <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem5" title="Definition 2.5 (Hankel Matrix). ‣ 2.3 In-Context Time Series Forecasting ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Definition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.5</span></a>, the final column of <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is the prediction token,</p>
<table id="A5.Ex38" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex38.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="x^{(n-p+1)}\;=\;\begin{bmatrix}x_{\,n-p+1:n}\\[2.0pt]
0\end{bmatrix},\qquad x_{\,n-p+1:n}\in\mdmathbb{R}^{p}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>R</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{(n-p+1)}\;=\;\begin{bmatrix}x_{\,n-p+1:n}\\[2.0pt]
0\end{bmatrix},\qquad x_{\,n-p+1:n}\in\mdmathbb{R}^{p}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">By <a href="#A5.Thmtheorem3" title="Lemma E.3 (Closed-form readout of one-layer LSA on Hankel input []). ‣ E.1 Fundamentals of Linear Self-Attention ‣ Appendix E Transformers Expressivity for TSF ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.3</span></a>, for suitable trainable <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P,Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P,Q</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="A5.Ex39" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex39.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\bigl[{\sf LSA}(H)\bigr]_{\,p+1,\,n-p+1}\;=\;b^{\top}\,G_{n}\,A\,x_{\,n-p+1:n},\qquad G_{n}\;:=\;\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{\,n-p}x^{(i)}x^{(i)\top}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mi>𝖫𝖲𝖠</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>A</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.558em">:=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></munderover><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em">⊤</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigl[{\sf LSA}(H)\bigr]_{\,p+1,\,n-p+1}\;=\;b^{\top}\,G_{n}\,A\,x_{\,n-p+1:n},\qquad G_{n}\;:=\;\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{\,n-p}x^{(i)}x^{(i)\top}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A5.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A5.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition E.6</span></span><span id="A5.Thmtheorem6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(LSA feature space)<span id="A5.Thmtheorem6.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A5.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For <math id="A5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j,r\in[p+1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j,r\in[p+1]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in[p]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in[p]</annotation></semantics></math>, define the cubic coordinates</span></p>
<table id="A5.Ex40" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex40.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\phi^{(p)}_{j,r,k}(x_{1:n})\;:=\;\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{\,n-p}x_{\,i+j-1}\,x_{\,i+r-1}\,x_{\,n-p+k}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">:=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></munderover><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi^{(p)}_{j,r,k}(x_{1:n})\;:=\;\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{\,n-p}x_{\,i+j-1}\,x_{\,i+r-1}\,x_{\,n-p+k}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.Thmtheorem6.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.Thmtheorem6.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Collect them into the feature map</span></p>
<table id="A5.Ex41" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex41.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Phi^{(p)}(x_{1:n})\;:=\;\bigl(\phi^{(p)}_{j,r,k}(x_{1:n})\bigr)_{j,r\in[p+1],\,k\in[p]}\in\mdmathbb{R}^{\,m},\qquad m\;=\;p\,(p+1)^{2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">:=</mo><msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mi>p</mi><msup><mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi^{(p)}(x_{1:n})\;:=\;\bigl(\phi^{(p)}_{j,r,k}(x_{1:n})\bigr)_{j,r\in[p+1],\,k\in[p]}\in\mdmathbb{R}^{\,m},\qquad m\;=\;p\,(p+1)^{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.Thmtheorem6.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.Thmtheorem6.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and define the linear self-attention feature space</span></p>
<table id="A5.Ex42" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex42.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{\mathrm{LSA}}^{(p)}\;:=\;\operatorname{span}\Bigl\{\phi^{(p)}_{j,r,k}(\,\cdot\,):\;j,r\in[p+1],\,k\in[p]\Bigr\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>LSA</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">:=</mo><mi>span</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo>⋅</mo><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">:</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{\mathrm{LSA}}^{(p)}\;:=\;\operatorname{span}\Bigl\{\phi^{(p)}_{j,r,k}(\,\cdot\,):\;j,r\in[p+1],\,k\in[p]\Bigr\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.Thmtheorem6.p1.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.Thmtheorem6.p1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Since each coordinate is a finite sum of degree-3 monomials, <math id="A5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi^{(p)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi^{(p)}</annotation></semantics></math> is continuous.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A5.Thmtheorem7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A5.Thmtheorem7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem E.7</span></span><span id="A5.Thmtheorem7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Representation of one-layer LSA readout)<span id="A5.Thmtheorem7.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A5.Thmtheorem7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.Thmtheorem7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.Thmtheorem7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There exist coefficients <math id="A5.Thmtheorem7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\beta_{j,r,k}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\beta_{j,r,k}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, depending on the trainable parameters in <math id="A5.Thmtheorem7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P,Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P,Q</annotation></semantics></math>, such that</span></p>
<table id="A5.Ex43" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex43.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\widehat{x}_{n+1}\;=\;\bigl[{\sf LSA}(H)\bigr]_{\,p+1,\,n-p+1}\;=\;\sum_{j=1}^{p+1}\sum_{r=1}^{p+1}\sum_{k=1}^{p}\beta_{j,r,k}\;\phi^{(p)}_{j,r,k}(x_{1:n})\;\in\;\mathcal{H}_{\mathrm{LSA}}^{(p)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mi>𝖫𝖲𝖠</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.391em">=</mo><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>p</mi></munderover><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">∈</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>LSA</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{x}_{n+1}\;=\;\bigl[{\sf LSA}(H)\bigr]_{\,p+1,\,n-p+1}\;=\;\sum_{j=1}^{p+1}\sum_{r=1}^{p+1}\sum_{k=1}^{p}\beta_{j,r,k}\;\phi^{(p)}_{j,r,k}(x_{1:n})\;\in\;\mathcal{H}_{\mathrm{LSA}}^{(p)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">By <a href="#A5.Thmtheorem3" title="Lemma E.3 (Closed-form readout of one-layer LSA on Hankel input []). ‣ E.1 Fundamentals of Linear Self-Attention ‣ Appendix E Transformers Expressivity for TSF ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.3</span></a>, for suitable trainable <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P,Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P,Q</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="A5.Ex44" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex44.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\bigl[{\sf LSA}(H)\bigr]_{\,p+1,\,n-p+1}=b^{\top}G_{n}Ax_{\,n-p+1:n},\qquad G_{n}:=\tfrac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{\,n-p}x^{(i)}x^{(i)\top}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mi>𝖫𝖲𝖠</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>A</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></munderover><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em">⊤</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigl[{\sf LSA}(H)\bigr]_{\,p+1,\,n-p+1}=b^{\top}G_{n}Ax_{\,n-p+1:n},\qquad G_{n}:=\tfrac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{\,n-p}x^{(i)}x^{(i)\top}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">Expanding the product gives</span></p>
<table id="A5.Ex45" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex45.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="b^{\top}G_{n}Ax_{n-p+1:n}=\sum_{j=1}^{p+1}\sum_{r=1}^{p+1}\sum_{k=1}^{p}(b_{j}A_{r,k})\;\phi^{(p)}_{j,r,k}(x_{1:n})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>A</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>p</mi></munderover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b^{\top}G_{n}Ax_{n-p+1:n}=\sum_{j=1}^{p+1}\sum_{r=1}^{p+1}\sum_{k=1}^{p}(b_{j}A_{r,k})\;\phi^{(p)}_{j,r,k}(x_{1:n}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.3.1" class="ltx_text">where we define <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{j,r,k}:=b_{j}A_{r,k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{j,r,k}:=b_{j}A_{r,k}</annotation></semantics></math>. This matches the claimed representation.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A5.Thmtheorem8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A5.Thmtheorem8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem E.8</span></span><span id="A5.Thmtheorem8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Risk monotonicity under LSA features)<span id="A5.Thmtheorem8.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A5.Thmtheorem8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.Thmtheorem8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.Thmtheorem8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="A5.Thmtheorem8.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(x_{1:n},x_{n+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{1:n},x_{n+1})</annotation></semantics></math> be jointly defined random variables. Under squared loss,</span></p>
<table id="A5.Ex46" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex46.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\inf_{f}\;\mdmathbb{E}\left[\bigl(x_{n+1}-f(x_{1:n})\bigr)^{2}\right]\;\leq\;\inf_{g}\;\mdmathbb{E}\left[\bigl(x_{n+1}-g(\Phi^{(p)}(x_{1:n}))\bigr)^{2}\right]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">inf</mo><mi>f</mi></munder><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo rspace="0.280em">]</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">inf</mo><mi>g</mi></munder><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\inf_{f}\;\mdmathbb{E}\left[\bigl(x_{n+1}-f(x_{1:n})\bigr)^{2}\right]\;\leq\;\inf_{g}\;\mdmathbb{E}\left[\bigl(x_{n+1}-g(\Phi^{(p)}(x_{1:n}))\bigr)^{2}\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.Thmtheorem8.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.Thmtheorem8.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Equality holds if and only if <math id="A5.Thmtheorem8.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="x_{n+1}\perp\!\!\!\perp x_{1:n}\,|\,\Phi^{(p)}(x_{1:n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mpadded style="width:0.240em;" width="0.240em"><mo>⟂</mo></mpadded><mo>⟂</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{n+1}\perp\!\!\!\perp x_{1:n}\,|\,\Phi^{(p)}(x_{1:n})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">By optimality of conditional expectations, the minimizers are <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mdmathbb{E}[x_{n+1}\mid x_{1:n}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mdmathbb{E}[x_{n+1}\mid x_{1:n}]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mdmathbb{E}[x_{n+1}\mid\Phi^{(p)}(x_{1:n})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mdmathbb{E}[x_{n+1}\mid\Phi^{(p)}(x_{1:n})]</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi^{(p)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi^{(p)}</annotation></semantics></math> is a deterministic function of <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="x_{1:n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1:n}</annotation></semantics></math>, Doob–Dynkin (<a href="#A5.Thmtheorem4" title="Lemma E.4 (Doob–Dynkin []). ‣ E.2 Function Space Constraints of Linear Self-Attention ‣ Appendix E Transformers Expressivity for TSF ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.4</span></a>) gives <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\sigma(\Phi^{(p)}(x_{1:n}))\subseteq\sigma(x_{1:n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(\Phi^{(p)}(x_{1:n}))\subseteq\sigma(x_{1:n})</annotation></semantics></math>. Conditional expectation is the <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> projection, so <a href="#A5.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem E.5 (Projection Monotonicity in 𝐿^2). ‣ E.2 Function Space Constraints of Linear Self-Attention ‣ Appendix E Transformers Expressivity for TSF ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.5</span></a> yields the inequality; the equality condition is standard.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A5.Thmtheorem9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A5.Thmtheorem9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem E.9</span></span><span id="A5.Thmtheorem9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Single-layer LSA cannot beat the linear predictor under AR(<math id="A5.Thmtheorem9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>))<span id="A5.Thmtheorem9.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A5.Thmtheorem9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.Thmtheorem9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.Thmtheorem9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose <math id="A5.Thmtheorem9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{x_{t}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{x_{t}\}</annotation></semantics></math> follows a stationary AR(<math id="A5.Thmtheorem9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>) process with context <math id="A5.Thmtheorem9.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="x_{1:n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1:n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A5.Thmtheorem9.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq p</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="A5.Thmtheorem9.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{x}_{n+1}^{\mathrm{LSA}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>LSA</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{x}_{n+1}^{\mathrm{LSA}}</annotation></semantics></math> denote any one-layer LSA readout as in <a href="#A5.Thmtheorem7" title="Theorem E.7 (Representation of one-layer LSA readout). ‣ Functional form of the one-layer readout. ‣ E.2 Function Space Constraints of Linear Self-Attention ‣ Appendix E Transformers Expressivity for TSF ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.7</span></a>. Then</span></p>
<table id="A5.Ex47" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex47.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\inf\;\mdmathbb{E}\left[\bigl(\widehat{x}_{n+1}^{\mathrm{LSA}}-x_{n+1}\bigr)^{2}\right]\;\geq\;\inf_{w\in\mdmathbb{R}^{n}}\mdmathbb{E}\left[\bigl(w^{\top}x_{1:n}-x_{n+1}\bigr)^{2}\right]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0.447em">inf</mo><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>LSA</mi></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo rspace="0.280em">]</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">inf</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></munder><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\inf\;\mdmathbb{E}\left[\bigl(\widehat{x}_{n+1}^{\mathrm{LSA}}-x_{n+1}\bigr)^{2}\right]\;\geq\;\inf_{w\in\mdmathbb{R}^{n}}\mdmathbb{E}\left[\bigl(w^{\top}x_{1:n}-x_{n+1}\bigr)^{2}\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">By <a href="#A5.Thmtheorem7" title="Theorem E.7 (Representation of one-layer LSA readout). ‣ Functional form of the one-layer readout. ‣ E.2 Function Space Constraints of Linear Self-Attention ‣ Appendix E Transformers Expressivity for TSF ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.7</span></a>, <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{x}_{n+1}^{\mathrm{LSA}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>LSA</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{x}_{n+1}^{\mathrm{LSA}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a measurable function of <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Phi^{(p)}(x_{1:n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi^{(p)}(x_{1:n})</annotation></semantics></math>, hence its best risk is at least that of the Bayes predictor given <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Phi^{(p)}(x_{1:n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi^{(p)}(x_{1:n})</annotation></semantics></math>; <a href="#A5.Thmtheorem8" title="Theorem E.8 (Risk monotonicity under LSA features). ‣ Functional form of the one-layer readout. ‣ E.2 Function Space Constraints of Linear Self-Attention ‣ Appendix E Transformers Expressivity for TSF ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.8</span></a> then lower-bounds this by the Bayes risk given <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="x_{1:n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1:n}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Under AR(<math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>), <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mdmathbb{E}[x_{n+1}\mid x_{1:n}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mdmathbb{E}[x_{n+1}\mid x_{1:n}]</annotation></semantics></math> is linear in <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="x_{1:n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1:n}</annotation></semantics></math>, so the Bayes risk equals the optimal linear risk. Hence the claim.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.3 </span>Nested Transformer Feature Spaces and Risk Monotonicity in Time Series Prediction</h3>

<div id="A5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We take a function-space perspective: with Hankel inputs and a masked label slot, the one-layer LSA readout operates in a restricted feature class. As the sequence length grows, these features collapse to scaled copies of the last <math id="A5.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> lags, which clarifies both our Hankel design (<math id="A5.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p{+}1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p{+}1</annotation></semantics></math> rows) and why one-layer LSA cannot fundamentally beat linear regression on AR(<math id="A5.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<section id="A5.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Setup.</h5>

<div id="A5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Throughout this subsection we work under the AR(<math id="A5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>) model in Definition <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem2" title="Definition 2.2 (AR(𝑝) Process []). ‣ 2.1 Time Series ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>, the Hankel construction in Definition <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem5" title="Definition 2.5 (Hankel Matrix). ‣ 2.3 In-Context Time Series Forecasting ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.5</span></a>, the one-layer LSA update in Definition <a href="#A5.Thmtheorem1" title="Definition E.1 (Linear Self-Attention (single layer)). ‣ E.1 Fundamentals of Linear Self-Attention ‣ Appendix E Transformers Expressivity for TSF ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.1</span></a>, and the cubic coordinates in Definition <a href="#A5.Thmtheorem6" title="Definition E.6 (LSA feature space). ‣ Functional form of the one-layer readout. ‣ E.2 Function Space Constraints of Linear Self-Attention ‣ Appendix E Transformers Expressivity for TSF ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.6</span></a>. Let <math id="A5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> be the mask from Definition <a href="#A5.Thmtheorem1" title="Definition E.1 (Linear Self-Attention (single layer)). ‣ E.1 Fundamentals of Linear Self-Attention ‣ Appendix E Transformers Expressivity for TSF ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.Thmtheorem10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A5.Thmtheorem10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem E.10</span></span><span id="A5.Thmtheorem10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Ergodic Theorem, Birkhoff <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>]</cite>)<span id="A5.Thmtheorem10.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A5.Thmtheorem10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.Thmtheorem10.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.Thmtheorem10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="A5.Thmtheorem10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi</annotation></semantics></math> be a random element in a measurable space <math id="A5.Thmtheorem10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> with distribution <math id="A5.Thmtheorem10.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="A5.Thmtheorem10.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T:S\to S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T:S\to S</annotation></semantics></math> be a <math id="A5.Thmtheorem10.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>-preserving transformation with invariant <math id="A5.Thmtheorem10.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>-field <math id="A5.Thmtheorem10.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then for any measurable function <math id="A5.Thmtheorem10.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="A5.Thmtheorem10.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</span></p>
<table id="A5.Ex48" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex48.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{n}\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}f(T^{k}\xi)\xrightarrow{\text{a.s.}}\mdmathbb{E}[f(\xi)\mid\mathcal{I}]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic" mathsize="0.700em">a.s.</mtext></mover><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{n}\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}f(T^{k}\xi)\xrightarrow{\text{a.s.}}\mdmathbb{E}[f(\xi)\mid\mathcal{I}],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.Thmtheorem10.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.Thmtheorem10.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where the convergence is almost surely.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A5.Thmtheorem11" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A5.Thmtheorem11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma E.11</span></span><span id="A5.Thmtheorem11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Asymptotic feature collapse)<span id="A5.Thmtheorem11.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A5.Thmtheorem11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.Thmtheorem11.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.Thmtheorem11.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Define the normalized masked Hankel Gram</span></p>
<table id="A5.Ex49" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex49.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{n}\;:=\;\frac{1}{n}\,H\,M\,H^{\top}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">:=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{n}\;:=\;\frac{1}{n}\,H\,M\,H^{\top}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.Thmtheorem11.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.Thmtheorem11.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Then, entrywise, <math id="A5.Thmtheorem11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{n}\xrightarrow{\text{a.s.}}\Gamma_{p+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic" mathsize="0.700em">a.s.</mtext></mover><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{n}\xrightarrow{\text{a.s.}}\Gamma_{p+1}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A5.Thmtheorem11.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="[\Gamma_{p+1}]_{ij}={}_{|i-j|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\Gamma_{p+1}]_{ij}={}_{|i-j|}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A5.Thmtheorem11.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{h}=\mdmathbb{E}[x_{t}x_{t+h}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mmultiscripts><mo>=</mo><mprescripts></mprescripts><mi>h</mi><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{h}=\mdmathbb{E}[x_{t}x_{t+h}]</annotation></semantics></math>.
Moreover, for the cubic coordinates <math id="A5.Thmtheorem11.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi^{(p)}_{j,r,k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi^{(p)}_{j,r,k}</annotation></semantics></math> in Definition <a href="#A5.Thmtheorem6" title="Definition E.6 (LSA feature space). ‣ Functional form of the one-layer readout. ‣ E.2 Function Space Constraints of Linear Self-Attention ‣ Appendix E Transformers Expressivity for TSF ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.6</span></a>,</span></p>
<table id="A5.Ex50" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex50.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\phi^{(p)}_{j,r,k}(x_{1:n})\;\xrightarrow{\text{a.s.}}\;{}_{|j-r|}\,x_{\,n-p+k},\qquad j,r\in[p{+}1],\ k\in[p]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic" mathsize="0.700em">a.s.</mtext></mover><mmultiscripts><mi>x</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi^{(p)}_{j,r,k}(x_{1:n})\;\xrightarrow{\text{a.s.}}\;{}_{|j-r|}\,x_{\,n-p+k},\qquad j,r\in[p{+}1],\ k\in[p].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.Thmtheorem11.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.Thmtheorem11.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Hence,</span></p>
<table id="A5.Ex51" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex51.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\operatorname{span}\bigl\{\phi^{(p)}_{j,r,k}(x_{1:n})\bigr\}\ \xrightarrow{\text{a.s.}}\ \operatorname{span}\{x_{\,n-p+1},\dots,x_{n}\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>span</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic" mathsize="0.700em">a.s.</mtext></mover><mi>span</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{span}\bigl\{\phi^{(p)}_{j,r,k}(x_{1:n})\bigr\}\ \xrightarrow{\text{a.s.}}\ \operatorname{span}\{x_{\,n-p+1},\dots,x_{n}\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Each entry of <math id="A5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is a time average of <math id="A5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i+a-1}x_{i+b-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i+a-1}x_{i+b-1}</annotation></semantics></math>; by Birkhoff’s ergodic theorem (Theorem <a href="#A5.Thmtheorem10" title="Theorem E.10 (Ergodic Theorem, Birkhoff []). ‣ Setup. ‣ E.3 Nested Transformer Feature Spaces and Risk Monotonicity in Time Series Prediction ‣ Appendix E Transformers Expressivity for TSF ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.10</span></a>), <math id="A5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{n}\to\Gamma_{p+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{n}\to\Gamma_{p+1}</annotation></semantics></math> almost surely. For <math id="A5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi^{(p)}_{j,r,k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi^{(p)}_{j,r,k}</annotation></semantics></math>, factor out <math id="A5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\,n-p+k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\,n-p+k}</annotation></semantics></math> and apply the same theorem to <math id="A5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tfrac{1}{n}\sum_{i}x_{\,i+j-1}x_{\,i+r-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tfrac{1}{n}\sum_{i}x_{\,i+j-1}x_{\,i+r-1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A5.Thmtheorem12" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A5.Thmtheorem12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary E.12</span></span><span id="A5.Thmtheorem12.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Nested spaces)<span id="A5.Thmtheorem12.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A5.Thmtheorem12.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.Thmtheorem12.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.Thmtheorem12.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="A5.Thmtheorem12.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{\mathrm{LSA}}^{(p)}:=\operatorname{span}\{x_{\,n-p+1},\dots,x_{n}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>LSA</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>span</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{\mathrm{LSA}}^{(p)}:=\operatorname{span}\{x_{\,n-p+1},\dots,x_{n}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then
<math id="A5.Thmtheorem12.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{\mathrm{LSA}}^{(p)}\subseteq\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{\mathrm{LSA}}^{(p+1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>LSA</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>⊆</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>LSA</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{\mathrm{LSA}}^{(p)}\subseteq\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{\mathrm{LSA}}^{(p+1)}</annotation></semantics></math>
for all <math id="A5.Thmtheorem12.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A5.Thmtheorem13" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A5.Thmtheorem13.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem E.13</span></span><span id="A5.Thmtheorem13.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Risk plateau on AR(<math id="A5.Thmtheorem13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>))<span id="A5.Thmtheorem13.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A5.Thmtheorem13.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.Thmtheorem13.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.Thmtheorem13.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Under AR(<math id="A5.Thmtheorem13.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>), the Bayes predictor is linear in the last <math id="A5.Thmtheorem13.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> lags:
<math id="A5.Thmtheorem13.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{n+1}=\sum_{j=1}^{p}\rho_{j}x_{\,n-j+1}+\varepsilon_{n+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">"</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{n+1}=\sum_{j=1}^{p}\rho_{j}x_{\,n-j+1}+\varepsilon_{n+1}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A5.Thmtheorem13.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mdmathbb{E}[\varepsilon_{n+1}\mid x_{1:n}]=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">"</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mdmathbb{E}[\varepsilon_{n+1}\mid x_{1:n}]=0</annotation></semantics></math>.
By Lemma <a href="#A5.Thmtheorem11" title="Lemma E.11 (Asymptotic feature collapse). ‣ Setup. ‣ E.3 Nested Transformer Feature Spaces and Risk Monotonicity in Time Series Prediction ‣ Appendix E Transformers Expressivity for TSF ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.11</span></a> and Corollary <a href="#A5.Thmtheorem12" title="Corollary E.12 (Nested spaces). ‣ Setup. ‣ E.3 Nested Transformer Feature Spaces and Risk Monotonicity in Time Series Prediction ‣ Appendix E Transformers Expressivity for TSF ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.12</span></a>, any one-layer LSA based on Hankel features operates (as <math id="A5.Thmtheorem13.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>) on <math id="A5.Thmtheorem13.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{span}\{x_{\,n-p+1},\dots,x_{n}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>span</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{span}\{x_{\,n-p+1},\dots,x_{n}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, the minimal achievable MSE equals the linear Bayes risk <math id="A5.Thmtheorem13.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\varepsilon}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">"</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\varepsilon}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and does not decrease for <math id="A5.Thmtheorem13.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\geq p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\geq p</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="A5.Ex52" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex52.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\inf_{f}\mdmathbb{E}\Bigl[(x_{n+1}-f(\text{LSA features of order }q))^{2}\Bigr]=\sigma_{\varepsilon}^{2},\qquad\forall\,q\geq p." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">inf</mo><mi>f</mi></munder><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">LSA features of order </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">"</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">∀</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\inf_{f}\mdmathbb{E}\Bigl[(x_{n+1}-f(\text{LSA features of order }q))^{2}\Bigr]=\sigma_{\varepsilon}^{2},\qquad\forall\,q\geq p.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A5.Thmtheorem14" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A5.Thmtheorem14.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition E.14</span></span><span id="A5.Thmtheorem14.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Asymptotic exact recovery: a constructive parameter choice)<span id="A5.Thmtheorem14.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A5.Thmtheorem14.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.Thmtheorem14.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.Thmtheorem14.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let the one-layer readout at the prediction slot be written as in <a href="#A5.Thmtheorem3" title="Lemma E.3 (Closed-form readout of one-layer LSA on Hankel input []). ‣ E.1 Fundamentals of Linear Self-Attention ‣ Appendix E Transformers Expressivity for TSF ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.3</span></a>:</span></p>
<table id="A5.Ex53" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex53.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\bigl[{\sf LSA}(H)\bigr]_{\,p+1,\,n-p+1}\;=\;b^{\top}G_{n}A\,x_{\,n-p+1:n}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mi>𝖫𝖲𝖠</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>A</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigl[{\sf LSA}(H)\bigr]_{\,p+1,\,n-p+1}\;=\;b^{\top}G_{n}A\,x_{\,n-p+1:n},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.Thmtheorem14.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.Thmtheorem14.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">with <math id="A5.Thmtheorem14.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\in\mdmathbb{R}^{p+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\in\mdmathbb{R}^{p+1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A5.Thmtheorem14.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\in\mdmathbb{R}^{(p+1)\times p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\in\mdmathbb{R}^{(p+1)\times p}</annotation></semantics></math>. Define</span></p>
<table id="A5.Ex54" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex54.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b^{*}\;:=\;(0,\dots,0,1)^{\top}\in\mdmathbb{R}^{p+1},\qquad A^{*}\;:=\;\begin{bmatrix}J\,\Gamma_{p}^{-1}\\[2.0pt]
\mathbf{0}_{1\times p}\end{bmatrix}\in\mdmathbb{R}^{(p+1)\times p}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">:=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.558em">:=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b^{*}\;:=\;(0,\dots,0,1)^{\top}\in\mdmathbb{R}^{p+1},\qquad A^{*}\;:=\;\begin{bmatrix}J\,\Gamma_{p}^{-1}\\[2.0pt]
\mathbf{0}_{1\times p}\end{bmatrix}\in\mdmathbb{R}^{(p+1)\times p},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.Thmtheorem14.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.Thmtheorem14.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="A5.Thmtheorem14.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J\in\mdmathbb{R}^{p\times p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J\in\mdmathbb{R}^{p\times p}</annotation></semantics></math> is the anti-diagonal permutation (reversal) matrix and <math id="A5.Thmtheorem14.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> is the <math id="A5.Thmtheorem14.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\times p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\times p</annotation></semantics></math> autocovariance Toeplitz matrix.
Then, as <math id="A5.Thmtheorem14.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="A5.Ex55" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex55.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="b^{*\top}G_{n}A^{*}\,x_{\,n-p+1:n}\;\xrightarrow{\text{a.s.}}\;\rho^{\top}x_{\,n-p+1:n}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>∗</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>⊤</mo></mrow></msup><mi>G</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>A</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic" mathsize="0.700em">a.s.</mtext></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b^{*\top}G_{n}A^{*}\,x_{\,n-p+1:n}\;\xrightarrow{\text{a.s.}}\;\rho^{\top}x_{\,n-p+1:n},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.Thmtheorem14.p1.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.Thmtheorem14.p1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i.e., the one-layer LSA readout exactly recovers the Bayes-optimal linear predictor in the limit.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">By Lemma <a href="#A5.Thmtheorem11" title="Lemma E.11 (Asymptotic feature collapse). ‣ Setup. ‣ E.3 Nested Transformer Feature Spaces and Risk Monotonicity in Time Series Prediction ‣ Appendix E Transformers Expressivity for TSF ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.11</span></a>, <math id="A5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{n}\to\Gamma_{p+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{n}\to\Gamma_{p+1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="A5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p3.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="b^{*\top}\Gamma_{p+1}=[{}_{p},\dots,{}_{1},{}_{0}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mrow><mo>∗</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>⊤</mo></mrow></msup><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mmultiscripts><mo>,</mo><mprescripts></mprescripts><mi>p</mi><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mmultiscripts><mo>,</mo><mprescripts></mprescripts><mn>1</mn><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mmultiscripts><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mprescripts></mprescripts><mn>0</mn><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b^{*\top}\Gamma_{p+1}=[{}_{p},\dots,{}_{1},{}_{0}]</annotation></semantics></math>, we get</span></p>
<table id="A5.Ex56" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex56.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="b^{*\top}\Gamma_{p+1}A^{*}\;=\;[{}_{p},\dots,{}_{1}]\,J\,\Gamma_{p}^{-1}\;=\;[{}_{1},\dots,{}_{p}]\,\Gamma_{p}^{-1}\;=\;\rho^{\top}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mrow><mo>∗</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>⊤</mo></mrow></msup><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mmultiscripts><mo>,</mo><mprescripts></mprescripts><mi>p</mi><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mmultiscripts><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">]</mo><mprescripts></mprescripts><mn>1</mn><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mrow><mi>J</mi><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mmultiscripts><mo>,</mo><mprescripts></mprescripts><mn>1</mn><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mmultiscripts><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">]</mo><mprescripts></mprescripts><mi>p</mi><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b^{*\top}\Gamma_{p+1}A^{*}\;=\;[{}_{p},\dots,{}_{1}]\,J\,\Gamma_{p}^{-1}\;=\;[{}_{1},\dots,{}_{p}]\,\Gamma_{p}^{-1}\;=\;\rho^{\top},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text">using the Yule–Walker relation <math id="A5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho=\Gamma_{p}^{-1}\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">
</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho=\Gamma_{p}^{-1}\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p3.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\gamma=({}_{1},\dots,{}_{p})^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">
</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mmultiscripts><mo>,</mo><mprescripts></mprescripts><mn>1</mn><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mmultiscripts><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mprescripts></mprescripts><mi>p</mi><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=({}_{1},\dots,{}_{p})^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS3.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Why exactly <math id="A5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p{+}1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p{+}1</annotation></semantics></math> Hankel rows.</h5>

<div id="A5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The first <math id="A5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> rows are necessary to capture the true <math id="A5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> lags of the AR(<math id="A5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>) process, while the <math id="A5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p{+}1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p{+}1)</annotation></semantics></math>-st row serves as the masked prediction slot. With fewer than <math id="A5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p{+}1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p{+}1</annotation></semantics></math> rows, the model cannot access all <math id="A5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> relevant lags. With more than <math id="A5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p{+}1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p{+}1</annotation></semantics></math> rows, the extra rows are asymptotically redundant, since the optimal predictor ultimately depends only on the last <math id="A5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> lags.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A6" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix F </span>Closed-Form Gap Characterization between LSA and Linear Models</h2>

<section id="A6.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">F.1 </span>Warm Up via AR(1)</h3>

<div id="A6.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Warm-up, not global optimum.</span>
Motivated by the asymptotic constructive choice in Proposition <a href="#A5.Thmtheorem14" title="Proposition E.14 (Asymptotic exact recovery: a constructive parameter choice). ‣ Setup. ‣ E.3 Nested Transformer Feature Spaces and Risk Monotonicity in Time Series Prediction ‣ Appendix E Transformers Expressivity for TSF ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.14</span></a> (where <math id="A6.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> has the last entry <math id="A6.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> and the last row of <math id="A6.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="A6.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>), we study the univariate AR(1) case and <em id="A6.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">restrict</em> <math id="A6.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(b,A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(b,A)</annotation></semantics></math> to the one-dimensional ray induced by that limiting structure. This gives a computable warm start that illustrates how Isserlis’ theorem (Theorem <a href="#A6.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem F.3 (Isserlis’ Theorem (Wick’s Formula) []). ‣ Auxiliary lemmas used in ‣ F.1 Warm Up via AR(1) ‣ Appendix F Closed-Form Gap Characterization between LSA and Linear Models ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F.3</span></a>) enters the calculation; it is not the global optimum over all <math id="A6.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(b,A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(b,A)</annotation></semantics></math>, but it tends to linear regression as <math id="A6.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A6.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A6.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition F.1</span></span><span id="A6.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(AR(1) warm start along the asymptotic ray)<span id="A6.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A6.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="A6.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{x_{t}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{x_{t}\}</annotation></semantics></math> be the stationary Gaussian <math id="A6.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{AR}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>AR</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{AR}(1)</annotation></semantics></math> process of Definition <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem2" title="Definition 2.2 (AR(𝑝) Process []). ‣ 2.1 Time Series ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>:</span></p>
<table id="A6.Ex57" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A6.Ex57.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}=\rho\,x_{t-1}+\varepsilon_{t},\quad|\rho|&lt;1,\quad\varepsilon_{t}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\mathrm{i.i.d.}}}{{\sim}}\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma_{\varepsilon}^{2})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">"</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">"</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mover><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mover><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">"</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}=\rho\,x_{t-1}+\varepsilon_{t},\quad|\rho|&lt;1,\quad\varepsilon_{t}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\mathrm{i.i.d.}}}{{\sim}}\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma_{\varepsilon}^{2}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A6.Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and set <math id="A6.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}:=\sigma_{\varepsilon}^{2}/(1-\rho^{2})=\mathrm{var}(x_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">"</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>var</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}:=\sigma_{\varepsilon}^{2}/(1-\rho^{2})=\mathrm{var}(x_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>.
For context <math id="A6.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A6.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="A6.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> be the Hankel matrix (Definition <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem5" title="Definition 2.5 (Hankel Matrix). ‣ 2.3 In-Context Time Series Forecasting ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.5</span></a>) with mask <math id="A6.Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=\mathrm{diag}(I_{\,n-1},0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>diag</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=\mathrm{diag}(I_{\,n-1},0)</annotation></semantics></math> and define the normalized Gram</span></p>
<table id="A6.Ex58" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A6.Ex58.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{n}:=\frac{1}{n}\,H_{n}MH_{n}^{\top}=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}\begin{pmatrix}x_{i}^{2}&amp;x_{i}x_{i+1}\\[2.0pt]
x_{i}x_{i+1}&amp;x_{i+1}^{2}\end{pmatrix}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mtd><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mtd><mtd><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{n}:=\frac{1}{n}\,H_{n}MH_{n}^{\top}=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}\begin{pmatrix}x_{i}^{2}&amp;x_{i}x_{i+1}\\[2.0pt]
x_{i}x_{i+1}&amp;x_{i+1}^{2}\end{pmatrix}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A6.Thmtheorem1.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.Thmtheorem1.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="A6.Thmtheorem1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{n}:=\tfrac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}x_{i}x_{i+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{n}:=\tfrac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}x_{i}x_{i+1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Restrict <math id="A6.Thmtheorem1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(b,A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(b,A)</annotation></semantics></math> to</span></p>
<table id="A6.Ex59" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A6.Ex59.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b=\begin{bmatrix}0\\[2.0pt]
1\end{bmatrix},\qquad A=\begin{bmatrix}\alpha\\[2.0pt]
0\end{bmatrix},\qquad\alpha\in\mdmathbb{R}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b=\begin{bmatrix}0\\[2.0pt]
1\end{bmatrix},\qquad A=\begin{bmatrix}\alpha\\[2.0pt]
0\end{bmatrix},\qquad\alpha\in\mdmathbb{R},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A6.Thmtheorem1.p1.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.Thmtheorem1.p1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">so the one-layer LSA readout at the prediction slot is</span></p>
<table id="A6.Ex60" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A6.Ex60.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{y}_{n}(\alpha)=(b^{\top}G_{n}A)\,x_{n}=\alpha\,S_{n}\,x_{n}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{y}_{n}(\alpha)=(b^{\top}G_{n}A)\,x_{n}=\alpha\,S_{n}\,x_{n}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A6.Thmtheorem1.p1.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.Thmtheorem1.p1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Consider the surrogate loss against the AR(1) linear term:</span></p>
<table id="A6.Ex61" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A6.Ex61.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}(\alpha)=\mdmathbb E[(\widehat{y}_{n}(\alpha)-\rho x_{n})^{2}]=\mdmathbb{E}[x_{n}^{2}(\alpha S_{n}-\rho)^{2}]=\alpha^{2}D_{n}-2\alpha\rho N_{n}+\rho^{2}\sigma^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}(\alpha)=\mdmathbb E[(\widehat{y}_{n}(\alpha)-\rho x_{n})^{2}]=\mdmathbb{E}[x_{n}^{2}(\alpha S_{n}-\rho)^{2}]=\alpha^{2}D_{n}-2\alpha\rho N_{n}+\rho^{2}\sigma^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A6.Thmtheorem1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.Thmtheorem1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.Thmtheorem1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Define the geometric sums (for <math id="A6.Thmtheorem1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>)</span></p>
<table id="A6.Ex62" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A6.Ex62.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{m}:=\sum_{k=1}^{m}\rho^{2k}=\frac{\rho^{2}(1-\rho^{2m})}{1-\rho^{2}},\qquad H_{m}:=\sum_{k=1}^{m}k\,\rho^{2k}=\frac{\rho^{2}\big(1-(m+1)\rho^{2m}+m\rho^{2(m+1)}\big)}{(1-\rho^{2})^{2}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></munderover><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></munderover><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{m}:=\sum_{k=1}^{m}\rho^{2k}=\frac{\rho^{2}(1-\rho^{2m})}{1-\rho^{2}},\qquad H_{m}:=\sum_{k=1}^{m}k\,\rho^{2k}=\frac{\rho^{2}\big(1-(m+1)\rho^{2m}+m\rho^{2(m+1)}\big)}{(1-\rho^{2})^{2}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A6.Thmtheorem1.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.Thmtheorem1.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Then, with <math id="A6.Thmtheorem1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{k}:=\mathrm{Cov}(x_{t},x_{t+k})=\sigma^{2}\rho^{|k|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>Cov</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{k}:=\mathrm{Cov}(x_{t},x_{t+k})=\sigma^{2}\rho^{|k|}</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="A6.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A6.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A6.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle N_{n}:=\mdmathbb E[x_{n}^{2}S_{n}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle N_{n}:=\mdmathbb E[x_{n}^{2}S_{n}]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A6.E2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{\sigma^{4}}{n}\Bigl[(n-1)\rho+\frac{2}{\rho}\,K_{n-1}\Bigr]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{\sigma^{4}}{n}\Bigl[(n-1)\rho+\frac{2}{\rho}\,K_{n-1}\Bigr],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A6.Ex63"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A6.Ex63.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle D_{n}:=\mdmathbb E[x_{n}^{2}S_{n}^{2}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle D_{n}:=\mdmathbb E[x_{n}^{2}S_{n}^{2}]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A6.Ex63.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{\sigma^{6}}{n^{2}}\Bigl[(n-1)n\,\rho^{2}+(n-1)+\bigl(4(n-1)-2\bigr)K_{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mn>6</mn></msup><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{\sigma^{6}}{n^{2}}\Bigl[(n-1)n\,\rho^{2}+(n-1)+\bigl(4(n-1)-2\bigr)K_{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A6.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A6.E3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\qquad\qquad+\bigl(4(n-1)+2\bigr)K_{n-2}+8H_{n-1}+4H_{n-2}\Bigr]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>8</mn><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">]</mo><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\qquad\qquad+\bigl(4(n-1)+2\bigr)K_{n-2}+8H_{n-1}+4H_{n-2}\Bigr].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A6.Thmtheorem1.p2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.Thmtheorem1.p2.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Consequently the unique minimizer along this ray and its value are</span></p>
<table id="A6.Ex64" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A6.Ex64.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{*}=\frac{\rho\,N_{n}}{D_{n}},\qquad\min_{\alpha}\mathcal{L}(\alpha)=\rho^{2}\sigma^{2}-\frac{\rho^{2}N_{n}^{2}}{D_{n}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mfrac></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>N</mi><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{*}=\frac{\rho\,N_{n}}{D_{n}},\qquad\min_{\alpha}\mathcal{L}(\alpha)=\rho^{2}\sigma^{2}-\frac{\rho^{2}N_{n}^{2}}{D_{n}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A6.Thmtheorem1.p2.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.Thmtheorem1.p2.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Equivalently,</span></p>
<table id="A6.Ex65" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A6.Ex65.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min_{\alpha}\,\mdmathbb{E}\big[(\widehat{x}_{n+1}^{\mathrm{LSA}}-x_{n+1})^{2}\big]=\sigma_{\varepsilon}^{2}+\rho^{2}\sigma^{2}-\frac{\rho^{2}N_{n}^{2}}{D_{n}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi></munder><mo lspace="0.337em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>LSA</mi></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">"</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>N</mi><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min_{\alpha}\,\mdmathbb{E}\big[(\widehat{x}_{n+1}^{\mathrm{LSA}}-x_{n+1})^{2}\big]=\sigma_{\varepsilon}^{2}+\rho^{2}\sigma^{2}-\frac{\rho^{2}N_{n}^{2}}{D_{n}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A6.Thmtheorem1.p2.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.Thmtheorem1.p2.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and, by the law of large numbers and dominated convergence,</span></p>
<table id="A6.Ex66" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A6.Ex66.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{*}\xrightarrow[n\to\infty]{}\frac{1}{\sigma^{2}},\qquad\min_{\alpha}\mathcal{L}(\alpha)\xrightarrow[n\to\infty]{}0," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><munderover accent="true" accentunder="true"><mo>→</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">n</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em" mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mphantom></mphantom></munderover><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><munderover accent="true" accentunder="true"><mo>→</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">n</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em" mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mphantom></mphantom></munderover><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{*}\xrightarrow[n\to\infty]{}\frac{1}{\sigma^{2}},\qquad\min_{\alpha}\mathcal{L}(\alpha)\xrightarrow[n\to\infty]{}0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A6.Thmtheorem1.p2.6" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.Thmtheorem1.p2.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i.e., this warm start tends to the linear-regression limit.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A6.SS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A6.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Step 1: <math id="A6.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>—pairwise expansion (4th order).<span id="A6.SS1.p2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text"></span></span></p>
<table id="A6.Ex67" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A6.Ex67.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{n}=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}\mdmathbb E[x_{n}^{2}x_{i}x_{i+1}]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{n}=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}\mdmathbb E[x_{n}^{2}x_{i}x_{i+1}].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A6.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.SS1.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">For the zero-mean Gaussian quadruple <math id="A6.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{n},x_{n},x_{i},x_{i+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{n},x_{n},x_{i},x_{i+1})</annotation></semantics></math>, by Isserlis (Theorem <a href="#A6.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem F.3 (Isserlis’ Theorem (Wick’s Formula) []). ‣ Auxiliary lemmas used in ‣ F.1 Warm Up via AR(1) ‣ Appendix F Closed-Form Gap Characterization between LSA and Linear Models ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F.3</span></a>) the three pairings give</span></p>
<table id="A6.Ex68" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A6.Ex68.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{array}[]{lcl}\ (x_{n},x_{n})(x_{i},x_{i+1})&amp;\Rightarrow&amp;\Gamma_{0}\Gamma_{1}=\sigma^{4}\rho,\\[2.0pt]
\ (x_{n},x_{i})(x_{n},x_{i+1})&amp;\Rightarrow&amp;\Gamma_{n-i}\Gamma_{n-i-1}=\sigma^{4}\rho^{2n-2i-1},\\[2.0pt]
\ (x_{n},x_{i+1})(x_{n},x_{i})&amp;\Rightarrow&amp;\Gamma_{n-i-1}\Gamma_{n-i}=\sigma^{4}\rho^{2n-2i-1}.\end{array}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd><mo stretchy="false">⇒</mo></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd><mo stretchy="false">⇒</mo></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd><mo stretchy="false">⇒</mo></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{array}[]{lcl}\ (x_{n},x_{n})(x_{i},x_{i+1})&amp;\Rightarrow&amp;\Gamma_{0}\Gamma_{1}=\sigma^{4}\rho,\\[2.0pt]
\ (x_{n},x_{i})(x_{n},x_{i+1})&amp;\Rightarrow&amp;\Gamma_{n-i}\Gamma_{n-i-1}=\sigma^{4}\rho^{2n-2i-1},\\[2.0pt]
\ (x_{n},x_{i+1})(x_{n},x_{i})&amp;\Rightarrow&amp;\Gamma_{n-i-1}\Gamma_{n-i}=\sigma^{4}\rho^{2n-2i-1}.\end{array}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A6.SS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.SS1.p2.3.1" class="ltx_text">Hence <math id="A6.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mdmathbb E[x_{n}^{2}x_{i}x_{i+1}]=\sigma^{4}\bigl(\rho+2\rho^{2n-2i-1}\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mdmathbb E[x_{n}^{2}x_{i}x_{i+1}]=\sigma^{4}\bigl(\rho+2\rho^{2n-2i-1}\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math>, and summing over <math id="A6.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> yields (<a href="#A6.E2" title="Equation 2 ‣ Proposition F.1 (AR(1) warm start along the asymptotic ray). ‣ F.1 Warm Up via AR(1) ‣ Appendix F Closed-Form Gap Characterization between LSA and Linear Models ‣ Why Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>).</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A6.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Step 2: <math id="A6.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>—pairwise expansion (6th order).<span id="A6.SS1.p3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text"></span></span></p>
<table id="A6.Ex69" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A6.Ex69.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{n}=\frac{1}{n^{2}}\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}\sum_{j=1}^{n-1}\mdmathbb E[x_{n}^{2}x_{i}x_{i+1}x_{j}x_{j+1}]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{n}=\frac{1}{n^{2}}\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}\sum_{j=1}^{n-1}\mdmathbb E[x_{n}^{2}x_{i}x_{i+1}x_{j}x_{j+1}]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A6.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.SS1.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text">splits into diagonal (<math id="A6.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=j</annotation></semantics></math>) and off-diagonal (<math id="A6.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&lt;j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&lt;j</annotation></semantics></math>) parts.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A6.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="A6.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Diagonal <math id="A6.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=j</annotation></semantics></math>.</em><span id="A6.SS1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text"> With <math id="A6.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X:=x_{n},\ Y:=x_{i},\ Z:=x_{i+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X:=x_{n},\ Y:=x_{i},\ Z:=x_{i+1}</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="A6.Ex70" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A6.Ex70.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mdmathbb E[X^{2}Y^{2}Z^{2}]=\Gamma_{0}^{3}+2\Gamma_{XY}^{2}\Gamma_{0}+2\Gamma_{XZ}^{2}\Gamma_{0}+2\Gamma_{YZ}^{2}\Gamma_{0}+8\Gamma_{XY}\Gamma_{XZ}\Gamma_{YZ}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>Z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mn>0</mn><mn>3</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mdmathbb E[X^{2}Y^{2}Z^{2}]=\Gamma_{0}^{3}+2\Gamma_{XY}^{2}\Gamma_{0}+2\Gamma_{XZ}^{2}\Gamma_{0}+2\Gamma_{YZ}^{2}\Gamma_{0}+8\Gamma_{XY}\Gamma_{XZ}\Gamma_{YZ}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A6.SS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.SS1.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text">Substituting <math id="A6.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{0}=\sigma^{2},\ \Gamma_{XY}=\sigma^{2}\rho^{n-i},\ \Gamma_{XZ}=\sigma^{2}\rho^{n-i-1},\ \Gamma_{YZ}=\sigma^{2}\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{0}=\sigma^{2},\ \Gamma_{XY}=\sigma^{2}\rho^{n-i},\ \Gamma_{XZ}=\sigma^{2}\rho^{n-i-1},\ \Gamma_{YZ}=\sigma^{2}\rho</annotation></semantics></math> and summing over <math id="A6.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\dots,n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\dots,n-1</annotation></semantics></math> gives the diagonal contribution</span></p>
<table id="A6.Ex71" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A6.Ex71.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{6}\Bigl((n-1)+2\rho^{2}(n-1)+2\sum_{k=1}^{n-2}\rho^{2k}+10\sum_{k=1}^{n-1}\rho^{2k}\Bigr)=\sigma^{6}\Bigl((n-1)+2\rho^{2}(n-1)+2K_{n-2}+10K_{n-1}\Bigr)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mn>6</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></munderover><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mn>6</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{6}\Bigl((n-1)+2\rho^{2}(n-1)+2\sum_{k=1}^{n-2}\rho^{2k}+10\sum_{k=1}^{n-1}\rho^{2k}\Bigr)=\sigma^{6}\Bigl((n-1)+2\rho^{2}(n-1)+2K_{n-2}+10K_{n-1}\Bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A6.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="A6.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Off-diagonal <math id="A6.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&lt;j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&lt;j</annotation></semantics></math>.</em><span id="A6.SS1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text"> Let <math id="A6.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{1}:=x_{i},\ Y_{2}:=x_{i+1},\ Z_{1}:=x_{j},\ Z_{2}:=x_{j+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{1}:=x_{i},\ Y_{2}:=x_{i+1},\ Z_{1}:=x_{j},\ Z_{2}:=x_{j+1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Grouping the 15 pairings by how the two copies of <math id="A6.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> are paired gives the per–<math id="A6.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math> contributions listed below (the factor “<math id="A6.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times 2</annotation></semantics></math>” indicates the symmetric counterpart):</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A6.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<table id="A6.Ex72" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center">
<table id="A6.Ex72.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_markedasmath ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="A6.Ex72.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A6.Ex72.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;"><math id="A6.Ex72.m1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{n},x_{n})(Y_{1},Y_{2})(Z_{1},Z_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{n},x_{n})(Y_{1},Y_{2})(Z_{1},Z_{2})</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="A6.Ex72.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;"><math id="A6.Ex72.m1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Rightarrow\ \sigma^{6}\rho^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo rspace="0.778em" stretchy="false">⇒</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mn>6</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Rightarrow\ \sigma^{6}\rho^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></th></tr>
<tr id="A6.Ex72.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A6.Ex72.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;"><math id="A6.Ex72.m1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{n},x_{n})(Y_{1},Z_{1})(Y_{2},Z_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{n},x_{n})(Y_{1},Z_{1})(Y_{2},Z_{2})</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="A6.Ex72.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;"><math id="A6.Ex72.m1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Rightarrow\ \sigma^{6}\rho^{2(j-i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo rspace="0.778em" stretchy="false">⇒</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mn>6</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Rightarrow\ \sigma^{6}\rho^{2(j-i)}</annotation></semantics></math></th></tr>
<tr id="A6.Ex72.1.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A6.Ex72.1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;"><math id="A6.Ex72.m1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{n},x_{n})(Y_{1},Z_{2})(Y_{2},Z_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{n},x_{n})(Y_{1},Z_{2})(Y_{2},Z_{1})</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="A6.Ex72.1.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;"><math id="A6.Ex72.m1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Rightarrow\ \sigma^{6}\rho^{2(j-i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo rspace="0.778em" stretchy="false">⇒</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mn>6</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">�</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Rightarrow\ \sigma^{6}\rho^{2(j-i)}</annotation></semantics></math></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="A6.Ex72.1.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A6.Ex72.1.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;"><math id="A6.Ex72.m1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{(x_{n},Y_{1}),(x_{n},Y_{2})\},(Z_{1},Z_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{(x_{n},Y_{1}),(x_{n},Y_{2})\},(Z_{1},Z_{2})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A6.Ex72.1.4.1.1" class="ltx_text"> <math id="A6.Ex72.m1.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(\times 2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\times 2)</annotation></semantics></math></span></th>
